42
00:02:00,050 --> 00:02:01,670
So inference-- what is inference?

43
00:02:01,670 --> 00:02:04,490
OK, this is a little confusing if you have taken a statistics

44
00:02:04,490 --> 00:02:07,090
class because the definition of inference in statistics

45
00:02:07,090 --> 00:02:09,449
is different than in machine learning.

46
00:02:09,449 --> 00:02:12,290
So the statistics definition is that inference

47
00:02:12,290 --> 00:02:15,930
is about figuring out properties of a data distribution

48
00:02:15,930 --> 00:02:18,090
from samples from that distribution,

49
00:02:18,090 --> 00:02:21,612
or figuring out properties about a data generating process.

50
00:02:21,612 --> 00:02:24,070
Maybe it's not characterized as a probability distribution,

51
00:02:24,070 --> 00:02:25,903
but some generative process, trying

52
00:02:25,903 --> 00:02:27,570
to figure out properties of that process

53
00:02:27,570 --> 00:02:31,370
from samples from that process.

54
00:02:31,370 --> 00:02:34,410
So what do we call that in machine learning?

55
00:02:34,410 --> 00:02:37,250
It can go under different names, but maybe density estimation,

56
00:02:37,250 --> 00:02:40,132
or typically, it's some kind of learning or training.

57
00:02:40,132 --> 00:02:42,090
So the thing that they talk about in statistics

58
00:02:42,090 --> 00:02:43,930
as inferring the parameters that explain

59
00:02:43,930 --> 00:02:49,510
the data-generating process-- we call that training a model.

60
00:02:49,510 --> 00:02:52,210
So the ML definition of inference

61
00:02:52,210 --> 00:02:55,350
is what statisticians call prediction.

62
00:02:55,350 --> 00:02:58,990
It's using a model to make predictions about a new query

63
00:02:58,990 --> 00:03:03,270
point, so to make inferences, in the more colloquial sense,

64
00:03:03,270 --> 00:03:07,670
about the answer to a new question

65
00:03:07,670 --> 00:03:10,030
or the interpretation of a new image,

66
00:03:10,030 --> 00:03:12,850
or whatever data you're processing,

67
00:03:12,850 --> 00:03:16,430
using the model to answer questions about that data.

68
00:03:16,430 --> 00:03:19,610
So we're going to go with the ML definition.

69
00:03:19,610 --> 00:03:21,890
And that's the one that's becoming-- taking over.

70
00:03:21,890 --> 00:03:24,410
But statisticians will still complain about this,

71
00:03:24,410 --> 00:03:28,770
so be careful when you talk to statisticians

72
00:03:28,770 --> 00:03:33,610
So here's a outline for some of the topics that we'll see.

73
00:03:33,610 --> 00:03:36,210
Well, I'm only going to cover some of these today.

74
00:03:36,210 --> 00:03:40,990
But this is how I tried to parse the landscape of training

75
00:03:40,990 --> 00:03:44,010
machine learning models versus using them to make predictions,

76
00:03:44,010 --> 00:03:46,390
so training versus inference.

77
00:03:46,390 --> 00:03:48,990
And most of this course has been about the training side.

78
00:03:48,990 --> 00:03:51,790
And indeed, most of deep learning as a field

79
00:03:51,790 --> 00:03:54,940
has been about training models on data

80
00:03:54,940 --> 00:03:59,100
to learn to learn properties of that data.

81
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:02,260
And the use of those models at test time

82
00:04:02,260 --> 00:04:05,645
for what I'm calling inference has been a more minor subject.

83
00:04:05,645 --> 00:04:06,520
So far in this class.

84
00:04:06,520 --> 00:04:08,820
It's almost entirely been just a forward pass

85
00:04:08,820 --> 00:04:10,180
through the trained model.

86
00:04:10,180 --> 00:04:12,660
That was all you do at test time.

87
00:04:12,660 --> 00:04:15,240
But it turns out you can do much more than that at test time,

88
00:04:15,240 --> 00:04:18,420
and if you do, you'll get a lot of advantage.

89
00:04:18,420 --> 00:04:19,985
So we've seen some of these topics,

90
00:04:19,985 --> 00:04:21,860
but let me just quickly go over this diagram,

91
00:04:21,860 --> 00:04:25,240
we'll come back to it a few times in this lecture

92
00:04:25,240 --> 00:04:26,960
So we have two regimes.

93
00:04:26,960 --> 00:04:28,800
We have training and inference.

94
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:35,380
So training is what you do in the factory, at the company.

95
00:04:35,380 --> 00:04:38,460
It's what you do before you actually

96
00:04:38,460 --> 00:04:40,820
come across new problems in the wild.

97
00:04:40,820 --> 00:04:43,500
An inference is what you do at test time, in the wild,

98
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:45,208
on deployment, on device.

99
00:04:45,208 --> 00:04:47,500
It's what a robot would do as it walks around this room

100
00:04:47,500 --> 00:04:49,600
and understands what's going on.

101
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:51,720
So we have those two different phases.

102
00:04:51,720 --> 00:04:54,400
And by the way, these terms and these distinctions

103
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:56,575
are my own attempt at categorizing things.

104
00:04:56,575 --> 00:04:58,700
There's a lot of different ways of looking at this.

105
00:04:58,700 --> 00:05:00,400
These are not hard boundaries.

106
00:05:00,400 --> 00:05:03,160
A few of the terms could go under different categories.

107
00:05:03,160 --> 00:05:06,560
So training is also called learning.

108
00:05:06,560 --> 00:05:08,520
It's called statistical inference.

109
00:05:08,520 --> 00:05:09,920
And in machine learning, we might

110
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:11,960
call it amortized inference, meaning

111
00:05:11,960 --> 00:05:14,860
I'm going to be amortized-- inference

112
00:05:14,860 --> 00:05:17,880
is about solving a problem, and training

113
00:05:17,880 --> 00:05:20,680
learns a set of weights that directly solves the problem

114
00:05:20,680 --> 00:05:23,720
without having to go through the step-by-step reasoning process.

115
00:05:23,720 --> 00:05:29,800
So it's amortization of the more extended-thinking inference

116
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:32,080
process,

117
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:34,740
Inference might go into the prediction, or thinking,

118
00:05:34,740 --> 00:05:37,000
reasoning, cognition is, I have a new query,

119
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,000
and I want to understand what the answer will

120
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,210
be by thinking through things, as opposed

121
00:05:41,210 --> 00:05:42,960
to learning, which is more about coming up

122
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:46,200
with a direct, feedforward heuristic mapping from data

123
00:05:46,200 --> 00:05:48,700
to answers.

124
00:05:48,700 --> 00:05:50,630
But there's a lot in between.

125
00:05:50,630 --> 00:05:55,393
So the most extreme version of training is pre-training,

126
00:05:55,393 --> 00:05:57,810
and that's what most of this course has really been about.

127
00:05:57,810 --> 00:06:00,352
It's been about representation learning, generative modeling,

128
00:06:00,352 --> 00:06:01,770
coming up with foundation models,

129
00:06:01,770 --> 00:06:03,450
doing next-token prediction on the internet,

130
00:06:03,450 --> 00:06:05,992
coming up with a really good set of weights that is generally

131
00:06:05,992 --> 00:06:08,173
useful for a lot of tasks.

132
00:06:08,173 --> 00:06:09,590
But then we said that you can also

133
00:06:09,590 --> 00:06:12,270
take that pre-trained representation

134
00:06:12,270 --> 00:06:15,430
and apply it to your problem of interest by fine-tuning

135
00:06:15,430 --> 00:06:19,110
or transfer learning or adaptation of various kinds.

136
00:06:19,110 --> 00:06:21,770
So pre-training-- given a model-- sorry, given data,

137
00:06:21,770 --> 00:06:25,430
learn a model or a representation.

138
00:06:25,430 --> 00:06:30,510
The training part of post-training or adaptation

139
00:06:30,510 --> 00:06:33,430
would be, given a model and new data, or a new task

140
00:06:33,430 --> 00:06:37,210
specification, update that model to solve that new task.

141
00:06:37,210 --> 00:06:39,342
And the main method for that is fine-tuning,

142
00:06:39,342 --> 00:06:41,550
but there's other methods like reinforcement learning

143
00:06:41,550 --> 00:06:42,925
from human feedback that we heard

144
00:06:42,925 --> 00:06:45,870
a bit about in Jacob's lecture.

145
00:06:45,870 --> 00:06:49,080
Then we're going to move over to the, upon deployment side.

146
00:06:49,080 --> 00:06:52,380
So this dotted line is meant to be, now I've taken my AI system,

147
00:06:52,380 --> 00:06:54,260
and it's not in the factory anymore.

148
00:06:54,260 --> 00:06:56,003
It's not in the server farm.

149
00:06:56,003 --> 00:06:56,920
It's out in the world.

150
00:06:56,920 --> 00:06:58,620
It's being used by a user.

151
00:06:58,620 --> 00:07:01,745
So at deployment time, we can also adapt a model's behavior.

152
00:07:01,745 --> 00:07:03,120
There's a few ways of doing that.

153
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:05,800
We could prompt it with different commands.

154
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:08,680
We could do in-context learning, which I'll mention again,

155
00:07:08,680 --> 00:07:11,722
but Jacob also talked about in his lecture.

156
00:07:11,722 --> 00:07:13,180
And then there's a few new concepts

157
00:07:13,180 --> 00:07:15,240
that I'll introduce-- test-time training.

158
00:07:15,240 --> 00:07:17,740
And there's a few concepts which I won't have time to get to

159
00:07:17,740 --> 00:07:19,940
but are also important, like any time

160
00:07:19,940 --> 00:07:23,780
you have a model that is using feedback in order

161
00:07:23,780 --> 00:07:26,820
to update its behavior, like a robot walking around and feeling

162
00:07:26,820 --> 00:07:30,040
its contact and the gravity, direction,

163
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:31,840
direction, and its torques and its joints,

164
00:07:31,840 --> 00:07:33,715
and so forth, it's going to use that feedback

165
00:07:33,715 --> 00:07:34,960
to change its behavior.

166
00:07:34,960 --> 00:07:37,180
So any time you have this feedback control,

167
00:07:37,180 --> 00:07:39,500
this is changing a model's behavior

168
00:07:39,500 --> 00:07:42,100
based on the test data itself.

169
00:07:42,100 --> 00:07:47,245
And then we have the most extreme side of inference,

170
00:07:47,245 --> 00:07:49,120
where we're not actually updating or changing

171
00:07:49,120 --> 00:07:50,380
the model's behavior at all.

172
00:07:50,380 --> 00:07:52,280
We're just using the model to search

173
00:07:52,280 --> 00:07:55,080
through a space of possibilities and find the best one.

174
00:07:55,080 --> 00:07:57,480
So given a model and a query, find the best answer

175
00:07:57,480 --> 00:07:58,237
to that query.

176
00:07:58,237 --> 00:08:00,320
And this is where we get to things that are really

177
00:08:00,320 --> 00:08:03,780
outside the realm of traditional deep learning,

178
00:08:03,780 --> 00:08:05,020
but now increasingly popular.

179
00:08:05,020 --> 00:08:07,320
This is more classical AI search methods,

180
00:08:07,320 --> 00:08:09,200
like tree search and breadth-first search

181
00:08:09,200 --> 00:08:14,840
and depth-first search and trial and error search and so forth.

182
00:08:14,840 --> 00:08:15,340
Cool.

183
00:08:15,340 --> 00:08:17,423
And then I'm going to, at the end of this lecture,

184
00:08:17,423 --> 00:08:19,640
talk a little bit about this interesting interplay

185
00:08:19,640 --> 00:08:24,520
between search and learning, where you can use search

186
00:08:24,520 --> 00:08:27,040
to learn new things, and you can use

187
00:08:27,040 --> 00:08:30,960
learning to speed up your search over the space of possibilities.

188
00:08:30,960 --> 00:08:37,323
And one thing I realized is that a very general-purpose name

189
00:08:37,323 --> 00:08:39,240
for all the methods that do that are basically

190
00:08:39,240 --> 00:08:40,700
reinforcement learning.

191
00:08:40,700 --> 00:08:42,112
So reinforcement learning-- we'll

192
00:08:42,112 --> 00:08:44,070
come back to this-- as the idea of using search

193
00:08:44,070 --> 00:08:45,590
to improve learning.

194
00:08:45,590 --> 00:08:47,510
But there's also some more modern things

195
00:08:47,510 --> 00:08:50,830
that are really exactly this, like the STAR method and then,

196
00:08:50,830 --> 00:08:52,890
presumably, the O1 method from OpenAI,

197
00:08:52,890 --> 00:08:55,510
but we don't really know.

198
00:08:55,510 --> 00:08:57,670
OK, so let me come back to "The Bitter Lesson"

199
00:08:57,670 --> 00:09:00,070
that I mentioned a few lectures ago.

200
00:09:00,070 --> 00:09:03,110
So "The Bitter Lesson," as you might recall,

201
00:09:03,110 --> 00:09:05,010
is this essay from Rich Sutton.

202
00:09:05,010 --> 00:09:08,310
And he said, the big lesson in AI

203
00:09:08,310 --> 00:09:13,550
has been that hand-designed systems with clever inductive

204
00:09:13,550 --> 00:09:16,490
biases and hand-wrought algorithms--

205
00:09:16,490 --> 00:09:18,270
they just don't work that well.

206
00:09:18,270 --> 00:09:20,090
There's only, really, two things that work.

207
00:09:20,090 --> 00:09:21,410
It's simple methods at scale.

208
00:09:21,410 --> 00:09:24,990
And the two that we know of are search and learning.

209
00:09:24,990 --> 00:09:27,890
So a lot of people have focused on the learning part.

210
00:09:27,890 --> 00:09:29,170
That's the deep learning idea.

211
00:09:29,170 --> 00:09:31,128
Just collect a lot of data, and train a network

212
00:09:31,128 --> 00:09:33,150
to imitate that data.

213
00:09:33,150 --> 00:09:36,910
But Sutton specified search as the other option,

214
00:09:36,910 --> 00:09:40,390
and so let's talk about what is search.

215
00:09:40,390 --> 00:09:44,170
And this is what's happening, really, in industry right

216
00:09:44,170 --> 00:09:47,750
now, is this move away from only focusing on train-time compute,

217
00:09:47,750 --> 00:09:52,890
but now using a lot of test-time compute or search at test time.

218
00:09:52,890 --> 00:09:58,170
So this is a plot from the blog post on the OpenAI O1

219
00:09:58,170 --> 00:10:00,290
model, their latest model.

220
00:10:00,290 --> 00:10:06,050
And what this plot is showing on the left

221
00:10:06,050 --> 00:10:10,490
is that as I train with more flops, I get better results.

222
00:10:10,490 --> 00:10:12,570
That's the scaling law.

223
00:10:12,570 --> 00:10:15,350
Train-time compute is I'm training on more data,

224
00:10:15,350 --> 00:10:17,570
or I'm doing more iterations of gradient descent,

225
00:10:17,570 --> 00:10:20,250
or this might, in fact, be some kind of RL training

226
00:10:20,250 --> 00:10:21,610
part of the pipeline.

227
00:10:21,610 --> 00:10:24,477
But whatever it is, if I train on more and more data,

228
00:10:24,477 --> 00:10:26,310
I'm going to get better and better accuracy,

229
00:10:26,310 --> 00:10:28,018
and there might be some kind of power law

230
00:10:28,018 --> 00:10:32,210
that relates my training time to my test performance.

231
00:10:32,210 --> 00:10:36,170
So notice the slope here is maybe 0.5.

232
00:10:36,170 --> 00:10:39,110
The really interesting thing is they said, well, wait a second.

233
00:10:39,110 --> 00:10:42,870
What if I scale up the amount of compute I apply at test time

234
00:10:42,870 --> 00:10:46,630
to do intelligent inference or search through my model?

235
00:10:46,630 --> 00:10:48,430
We'll talk about how that can be done.

236
00:10:48,430 --> 00:10:50,870
And they saw that you also can get a power law.

237
00:10:50,870 --> 00:10:52,950
And in fact, the slope in this particular case

238
00:10:52,950 --> 00:10:56,430
is even steeper than the train-time power law.

239
00:10:56,430 --> 00:10:59,070
So the big move that a lot of companies are making right now

240
00:10:59,070 --> 00:11:02,630
is from only focusing on deploying compute at train time

241
00:11:02,630 --> 00:11:05,552
to now deploying a lot more compute at test time, not just

242
00:11:05,552 --> 00:11:07,010
the forward pass through the model,

243
00:11:07,010 --> 00:11:10,590
but many, many passes through the model at test time.

244
00:11:10,590 --> 00:11:14,990
So that's the big change from a few years ago.

245
00:11:14,990 --> 00:11:16,450
OK, so what is search?

246
00:11:16,450 --> 00:11:20,210
I think a lot of you have taken Intro AI and Algorithms.

247
00:11:20,210 --> 00:11:21,670
So you know, basically, that search

248
00:11:21,670 --> 00:11:25,870
is methods from those classes, but let's go over it.

249
00:11:25,870 --> 00:11:29,550
So search is the way that I used to be done.

250
00:11:29,550 --> 00:11:35,150
So in 1994, maybe, Garry Kasparov

251
00:11:35,150 --> 00:11:39,860
was beaten by this chess machine called Deep Blue.

252
00:11:39,860 --> 00:11:45,600
And the way that AI worked was it was just based on search.

253
00:11:45,600 --> 00:11:48,020
It had very little or maybe no learning or anything

254
00:11:48,020 --> 00:11:49,478
you'd really want to call learning.

255
00:11:49,478 --> 00:11:51,840
It just did look-ahead search.

256
00:11:51,840 --> 00:11:53,840
It said, well, if Kasparov makes this move,

257
00:11:53,840 --> 00:11:54,940
then I could respond with this move,

258
00:11:54,940 --> 00:11:56,080
and then he might make that move,

259
00:11:56,080 --> 00:11:57,205
and I could make this move.

260
00:11:57,205 --> 00:12:00,260
And it looked through this chain of all the possibilities, 12,

261
00:12:00,260 --> 00:12:03,020
10, 20, 30 moves in advance.

262
00:12:03,020 --> 00:12:04,980
And it just made this huge, huge chain.

263
00:12:04,980 --> 00:12:07,325
The branching factor of that huge, huge tree--

264
00:12:07,325 --> 00:12:08,700
the branching factor of that tree

265
00:12:08,700 --> 00:12:10,820
was, in chess, however many moves

266
00:12:10,820 --> 00:12:13,980
you can make at each position in the chess game.

267
00:12:13,980 --> 00:12:15,700
So it turned out that chess does not

268
00:12:15,700 --> 00:12:17,080
have that big a branching factor,

269
00:12:17,080 --> 00:12:19,400
so you could enumerate all possible futures

270
00:12:19,400 --> 00:12:21,900
and just take the set of actions that will lead to the best

271
00:12:21,900 --> 00:12:23,900
possible future.

272
00:12:23,900 --> 00:12:26,820
So just searching over all the things that you could do.

273
00:12:26,820 --> 00:12:28,600
Any of you play chess-- you probably do this in your head.

274
00:12:28,600 --> 00:12:30,183
You think about all the possibilities,

275
00:12:30,183 --> 00:12:31,820
and then you pick the move that will

276
00:12:31,820 --> 00:12:35,160
maximize your expected reward against all those possibilities.

277
00:12:37,880 --> 00:12:41,360
OK, then some years later, AI systems

278
00:12:41,360 --> 00:12:44,320
won that Go with the AlphaGo system, which you've probably

279
00:12:44,320 --> 00:12:45,320
heard about.

280
00:12:45,320 --> 00:12:47,740
And AlphaGo also heavily uses search.

281
00:12:47,740 --> 00:12:51,460
But AlphaGo uses both search and learning.

282
00:12:51,460 --> 00:12:52,920
So it's one of these methods that

283
00:12:52,920 --> 00:12:54,560
uses a combination of the two.

284
00:12:54,560 --> 00:12:56,840
But I'm going to focus on the search part right now.

285
00:12:56,840 --> 00:12:58,960
So what they did is they, again, said

286
00:12:58,960 --> 00:13:00,260
here's a particular game state.

287
00:13:00,260 --> 00:13:03,640
Let's just expand out the tree of all possible futures.

288
00:13:03,640 --> 00:13:05,920
So after this game state, my opponent

289
00:13:05,920 --> 00:13:07,620
could move in these two locations.

290
00:13:07,620 --> 00:13:09,840
Maybe there's other locations they can move to.

291
00:13:09,840 --> 00:13:11,240
And then I'll descend the tree and say, well,

292
00:13:11,240 --> 00:13:13,360
if they move here, then here's what I should do.

293
00:13:13,360 --> 00:13:15,560
And I'll search through this space

294
00:13:15,560 --> 00:13:16,960
of all these possibilities.

295
00:13:16,960 --> 00:13:18,760
Now, the branching factor, the number

296
00:13:18,760 --> 00:13:21,080
of branches in that tree for the game of Go

297
00:13:21,080 --> 00:13:22,920
is much higher than the game of chess.

298
00:13:22,920 --> 00:13:25,382
And that's why they weren't able to do brute-force search

299
00:13:25,382 --> 00:13:26,340
like they did in chess.

300
00:13:26,340 --> 00:13:28,480
Instead, they had to do search with heuristics--

301
00:13:28,480 --> 00:13:31,060
with learned heuristics on top.

302
00:13:31,060 --> 00:13:34,510
We'll come back to that, too.

303
00:13:34,510 --> 00:13:36,870
OK, so there's this general observation

304
00:13:36,870 --> 00:13:39,550
that there's these two things that just get better

305
00:13:39,550 --> 00:13:40,410
with more compute.

306
00:13:40,410 --> 00:13:42,430
One is learning, and the other is search.

307
00:13:42,430 --> 00:13:46,190
And sometimes they trade off, and you can get a good AI system

308
00:13:46,190 --> 00:13:47,950
with one or the other.

309
00:13:47,950 --> 00:13:52,430
And this is an interesting paper from Jones

310
00:13:52,430 --> 00:13:56,090
on scaling laws of board games, where they actually said, well,

311
00:13:56,090 --> 00:13:58,678
we can solve chess either by taking a million chess

312
00:13:58,678 --> 00:14:00,470
games or a billion chess games and learning

313
00:14:00,470 --> 00:14:03,710
the mapping from the board state to the best action

314
00:14:03,710 --> 00:14:05,510
that Garry Kasparov would make.

315
00:14:05,510 --> 00:14:08,830
Or we could solve chess by just not having

316
00:14:08,830 --> 00:14:13,150
any pre-trained learning of optimal moves, but instead just,

317
00:14:13,150 --> 00:14:15,590
for every game position, search ahead in time

318
00:14:15,590 --> 00:14:17,470
and see that tree of possibilities,

319
00:14:17,470 --> 00:14:20,950
and take the move that maximizes the expected value.

320
00:14:20,950 --> 00:14:26,070
And there's a trade off, like, a Pareto front, like, I can--

321
00:14:26,070 --> 00:14:27,970
well, yeah, it's just a trade-off curve.

322
00:14:27,970 --> 00:14:30,510
It's saying that I can do more search,

323
00:14:30,510 --> 00:14:32,965
and then I can get the same elo for less training,

324
00:14:32,965 --> 00:14:34,340
or I can do less search, and then

325
00:14:34,340 --> 00:14:36,500
I can get the same elo for more training.

326
00:14:36,500 --> 00:14:40,860
So there's two different ways of being smart, search or learning.

327
00:14:40,860 --> 00:14:43,500
And sometimes it's better to use search

328
00:14:43,500 --> 00:14:46,840
because I don't have to spend as much time learning,

329
00:14:46,840 --> 00:14:48,680
and maybe it scales better in some sense.

330
00:14:48,680 --> 00:14:50,347
And sometimes it's better to do learning

331
00:14:50,347 --> 00:14:52,960
because in other situations, that might scale better.

332
00:14:52,960 --> 00:14:56,220
And also, learning means that I don't, at test time,

333
00:14:56,220 --> 00:14:57,560
have to do a lot of compute.

334
00:14:57,560 --> 00:14:59,060
So search is test time.

335
00:14:59,060 --> 00:15:01,440
Learning is train time.

336
00:15:01,440 --> 00:15:01,940
OK.

337
00:15:04,800 --> 00:15:06,260
So now let's talk about some search

338
00:15:06,260 --> 00:15:08,915
methods that are used in deep learning.

339
00:15:08,915 --> 00:15:10,540
And I'm going to talk about these three

340
00:15:10,540 --> 00:15:13,040
in particular, Best-of-N, beam search, and Chain-of-Thought.

341
00:15:13,040 --> 00:15:17,020
We already saw Chain-of-Thought a little bit.

342
00:15:17,020 --> 00:15:21,660
So the first thing that I want to convince you of

343
00:15:21,660 --> 00:15:24,920
is that the language models that we have,

344
00:15:24,920 --> 00:15:27,860
which are autoregressive models, can

345
00:15:27,860 --> 00:15:32,080
be improved by sampling multiple possible completions

346
00:15:32,080 --> 00:15:33,560
and picking the best one.

347
00:15:33,560 --> 00:15:35,060
So why can they be improved?

348
00:15:35,060 --> 00:15:38,440
Let's look at how these systems are trained.

349
00:15:38,440 --> 00:15:41,100
So this slide is a little bit old,

350
00:15:41,100 --> 00:15:42,980
so I was showing the example with an LSTM.

351
00:15:42,980 --> 00:15:44,563
You can replace it with a transformer,

352
00:15:44,563 --> 00:15:45,960
but it doesn't really matter.

353
00:15:45,960 --> 00:15:47,460
So let's just keep it as an LSTM.

354
00:15:47,460 --> 00:15:49,220
So here, we have a molecule.

355
00:15:49,220 --> 00:15:52,640
Anyone recognize what that molecule is?

356
00:15:52,640 --> 00:15:53,700
Somebody knows it.

357
00:15:53,700 --> 00:15:54,560
AUDIENCE: Caffeine.

358
00:15:54,560 --> 00:15:55,840
PHILLIP ISOLA: Caffeine, yes.

359
00:15:55,840 --> 00:15:58,900
So we're going to have a graph net that will process this.

360
00:15:58,900 --> 00:16:00,948
Maybe your final project, you're putting together

361
00:16:00,948 --> 00:16:02,740
different architectures that suit the data.

362
00:16:02,740 --> 00:16:04,668
So graph net makes sense on a molecule.

363
00:16:04,668 --> 00:16:06,960
And then we're going to have an LSTM that will spit out

364
00:16:06,960 --> 00:16:12,260
a sentence that describes, this is molecule to sentence,

365
00:16:12,260 --> 00:16:13,588
captioning a molecule.

366
00:16:13,588 --> 00:16:15,880
So it's going to say, oh, this is a mild stimulant that

367
00:16:15,880 --> 00:16:18,480
enhances cognitive abilities.

368
00:16:18,480 --> 00:16:20,560
That's caffeine.

369
00:16:20,560 --> 00:16:23,080
So here's how you train that system.

370
00:16:23,080 --> 00:16:26,700
We're going to have a data that we're imitating.

371
00:16:26,700 --> 00:16:28,725
This will be just generative modeling.

372
00:16:28,725 --> 00:16:30,100
So we're going to try to maximize

373
00:16:30,100 --> 00:16:32,540
the likelihood of the target data, which is just

374
00:16:32,540 --> 00:16:34,540
sentences from some corpus.

375
00:16:34,540 --> 00:16:37,740
And we will output a categorical distribution

376
00:16:37,740 --> 00:16:41,020
over the next token and a sample from that in order

377
00:16:41,020 --> 00:16:44,060
to produce sentences.

378
00:16:44,060 --> 00:16:45,980
So we're going to try to maximize

379
00:16:45,980 --> 00:16:48,660
the likelihood of the sentence, or the characters

380
00:16:48,660 --> 00:16:50,920
y at every point in this sequence.

381
00:16:54,460 --> 00:16:57,260
That other picture of that y is going

382
00:16:57,260 --> 00:16:59,838
to be a one-hot probability vector.

383
00:16:59,838 --> 00:17:01,380
And we're going to make a prediction.

384
00:17:01,380 --> 00:17:02,880
I'm going to take the cross entropy.

385
00:17:02,880 --> 00:17:05,339
That's just doing the pointwise product

386
00:17:05,339 --> 00:17:08,579
between the one-hot vector and the predicted probability

387
00:17:08,579 --> 00:17:10,300
vector.

388
00:17:10,300 --> 00:17:12,480
So that's cross-entropy minimization,

389
00:17:12,480 --> 00:17:16,263
so this is stuff you've seen before.

390
00:17:16,263 --> 00:17:17,180
Oh, the color changed.

391
00:17:17,180 --> 00:17:18,180
OK, anyway.

392
00:17:18,180 --> 00:17:22,579
So there's this interesting thing that you do.

393
00:17:22,579 --> 00:17:25,220
In transformers, this stopped being a term.

394
00:17:25,220 --> 00:17:26,970
But in the LSTM era, we used to talk

395
00:17:26,970 --> 00:17:29,773
about the way of training this thing as teacher forcing.

396
00:17:29,773 --> 00:17:31,190
So what does teacher forcing mean?

397
00:17:31,190 --> 00:17:34,680
Teacher forcing means I'm going to take the target sentence.

398
00:17:34,680 --> 00:17:36,930
I'm going to shift it by 1, just like I would normally

399
00:17:36,930 --> 00:17:39,930
do in a transformer training, an autoregressive model.

400
00:17:39,930 --> 00:17:43,450
And I'm actually going to tell the LSTM-- rather than just

401
00:17:43,450 --> 00:17:45,170
reading off the entire sentence, I

402
00:17:45,170 --> 00:17:48,770
will tell it the ground-truth previous word at each time step

403
00:17:48,770 --> 00:17:53,285
is whatever it was in the training data.

404
00:17:53,285 --> 00:17:55,410
So I'm going to condition each next-word prediction

405
00:17:55,410 --> 00:17:58,530
on all of the ground-truth previous words.

406
00:17:58,530 --> 00:18:01,810
And this is where you can see there's a deviation between how

407
00:18:01,810 --> 00:18:04,930
we'll use this system at test time and how it was trained.

408
00:18:04,930 --> 00:18:07,170
It's trained to predict the next word

409
00:18:07,170 --> 00:18:09,450
given the ground-truth previous words.

410
00:18:09,450 --> 00:18:11,430
But at test time, at deployment time,

411
00:18:11,430 --> 00:18:14,930
we're going to condition each next prediction

412
00:18:14,930 --> 00:18:17,210
on the model's own self-generated previous

413
00:18:17,210 --> 00:18:18,650
prediction.

414
00:18:18,650 --> 00:18:19,950
So that's a difference.

415
00:18:19,950 --> 00:18:23,350
That means that maximizing the likelihood on the training data

416
00:18:23,350 --> 00:18:28,890
will have a gap from what we really would want,

417
00:18:28,890 --> 00:18:32,950
which is to maximize the likelihood of entire sequences

418
00:18:32,950 --> 00:18:34,365
sampled from the model.

419
00:18:34,365 --> 00:18:35,990
So it's actually going to be maximizing

420
00:18:35,990 --> 00:18:37,870
the likelihood of the model's predictions

421
00:18:37,870 --> 00:18:40,365
of every next character or word, given

422
00:18:40,365 --> 00:18:42,990
the ground-truth previous words, which is not what you actually

423
00:18:42,990 --> 00:18:45,510
have at test time.

424
00:18:45,510 --> 00:18:47,590
So this teacher forcing way of training

425
00:18:47,590 --> 00:18:49,150
means that you're not actually going

426
00:18:49,150 --> 00:18:51,510
to be maximizing the likelihood of the caption given

427
00:18:51,510 --> 00:18:54,590
the data at test time, because here's what we do

428
00:18:54,590 --> 00:18:56,350
at inference or at test time.

429
00:18:56,350 --> 00:18:58,470
We sample from the predicted distribution

430
00:18:58,470 --> 00:18:59,330
over the next words.

431
00:18:59,330 --> 00:19:01,310
Or maybe we do greedy sampling, where we just

432
00:19:01,310 --> 00:19:04,310
take the most likely next token given the previous tokens.

433
00:19:04,310 --> 00:19:05,830
So first, we'll sample the word A,

434
00:19:05,830 --> 00:19:09,890
and then we'll condition the model on that we sampled A.

435
00:19:09,890 --> 00:19:11,670
So now what comes next?

436
00:19:11,670 --> 00:19:12,690
A strong and so forth.

437
00:19:12,690 --> 00:19:18,110
And this is how you sample from a autoregressive language model.

438
00:19:18,110 --> 00:19:20,018
And again, I'm showing it with an LSTM,

439
00:19:20,018 --> 00:19:22,060
but you do exactly the same thing in transformer.

440
00:19:22,060 --> 00:19:23,820
You condition every next-token prediction

441
00:19:23,820 --> 00:19:26,720
on all the previous sampled tokens.

442
00:19:26,720 --> 00:19:32,620
You're just writing one word at a time, or one token at a time.

443
00:19:32,620 --> 00:19:36,280
So now, because these are samples from the model,

444
00:19:36,280 --> 00:19:39,660
they might differ from the ground-truth distribution

445
00:19:39,660 --> 00:19:41,240
that the model was trained on.

446
00:19:41,240 --> 00:19:43,715
And there'll be this kind of drift that occurs.

447
00:19:43,715 --> 00:19:45,340
The model will produce something that's

448
00:19:45,340 --> 00:19:49,460
a little bit different than the true data distribution,

449
00:19:49,460 --> 00:19:52,940
and then its next predictions will be of distribution

450
00:19:52,940 --> 00:19:56,140
compared to how it was trained.

451
00:19:56,140 --> 00:19:58,580
OK.

452
00:19:58,580 --> 00:19:59,080
Yeah.

453
00:19:59,080 --> 00:20:01,560
So then it gets to the end.

454
00:20:01,560 --> 00:20:08,900
So the important point here is that autoregressive greedy

455
00:20:08,900 --> 00:20:10,220
sampling--

456
00:20:10,220 --> 00:20:13,760
the way that-- either greedy sampling or non-greedy sampling,

457
00:20:13,760 --> 00:20:16,060
but let's say autoregressive greedy sampling does not

458
00:20:16,060 --> 00:20:19,820
maximize the joint probability of the entire sequence.

459
00:20:19,820 --> 00:20:21,600
So remember, the autoregressive model

460
00:20:21,600 --> 00:20:24,440
is a model of the probability of a sequence of tokens.

461
00:20:24,440 --> 00:20:26,840
And it decomposes by this chain rule

462
00:20:26,840 --> 00:20:28,400
as probability of the first token,

463
00:20:28,400 --> 00:20:30,220
then the second one given the first one.

464
00:20:30,220 --> 00:20:31,960
This is the underlying probability model

465
00:20:31,960 --> 00:20:34,600
of autoregressive models.

466
00:20:34,600 --> 00:20:37,500
Now, I really want to find the sequence,

467
00:20:37,500 --> 00:20:39,920
in this case, the caption or the sentence,

468
00:20:39,920 --> 00:20:43,720
that maximizes the joint probability that my model

469
00:20:43,720 --> 00:20:46,040
assigns to the data.

470
00:20:46,040 --> 00:20:47,420
That will be some sequence.

471
00:20:47,420 --> 00:20:50,360
But with greedy sampling, I will first

472
00:20:50,360 --> 00:20:52,442
take the most likely first word.

473
00:20:52,442 --> 00:20:53,900
And then I'll say, given that word,

474
00:20:53,900 --> 00:20:56,440
take the most likely second word, and so on.

475
00:20:56,440 --> 00:21:00,240
This sequence, x1 star, x2 star, xn star,

476
00:21:00,240 --> 00:21:05,300
is not going to equal the optimal joint configuration.

477
00:21:05,300 --> 00:21:08,665
I've used the same notation, but those are not the same values.

478
00:21:08,665 --> 00:21:10,040
You can construct counterexamples

479
00:21:10,040 --> 00:21:12,800
where that's not the case.

480
00:21:12,800 --> 00:21:15,440
So an observation here is, could we

481
00:21:15,440 --> 00:21:17,970
do better than greedy sampling?

482
00:21:17,970 --> 00:21:19,590
Could we come up with a different way

483
00:21:19,590 --> 00:21:21,830
of sampling that gets higher likelihood

484
00:21:21,830 --> 00:21:23,670
configurations of the data?

485
00:21:23,670 --> 00:21:24,630
Yeah, question.

486
00:21:24,630 --> 00:21:26,050
AUDIENCE: So just to confirm what you said.

487
00:21:26,050 --> 00:21:27,550
It's not an equal sign, then, right?

488
00:21:27,550 --> 00:21:30,630
[INAUDIBLE] are not the same.

489
00:21:30,630 --> 00:21:33,510
PHILLIP ISOLA: Sorry, the equal sign is true,

490
00:21:33,510 --> 00:21:37,270
but the argmax solution is not the same

491
00:21:37,270 --> 00:21:40,230
for the argmax over the joint configuration, argmax

492
00:21:40,230 --> 00:21:42,150
over the individual conditionals.

493
00:21:42,150 --> 00:21:43,042
Yeah.

494
00:21:43,042 --> 00:21:45,750
Yeah, I probably should have had a prime or something to indicate

495
00:21:45,750 --> 00:21:47,125
that those were different values.

496
00:21:50,715 --> 00:21:54,640
So question for you to think about.

497
00:21:54,640 --> 00:21:56,390
So this is true for autoregressive models,

498
00:21:56,390 --> 00:22:00,030
that if you take the samples that

499
00:22:00,030 --> 00:22:03,837
maximize the sequence of conditional probabilities,

500
00:22:03,837 --> 00:22:05,670
you don't necessarily get the max likelihood

501
00:22:05,670 --> 00:22:07,950
joint configuration.

502
00:22:07,950 --> 00:22:09,170
What about for VAEs?

503
00:22:09,170 --> 00:22:13,510
What if you sample from a VAE and you take the argmax,

504
00:22:13,510 --> 00:22:15,230
the most likely sample-- like, you just--

505
00:22:15,230 --> 00:22:18,080
you encode, and now it's a Gaussian VAE,

506
00:22:18,080 --> 00:22:22,700
so the most likely sample is the mean of that VAE.

507
00:22:22,700 --> 00:22:24,500
Do you get the same issue?

508
00:22:24,500 --> 00:22:26,420
What do you think?

509
00:22:26,420 --> 00:22:27,920
This takes a little bit of thought.

510
00:22:31,340 --> 00:22:33,380
How about for a diffusion model?

511
00:22:33,380 --> 00:22:38,300
If I do denoising step by step, is my final output--

512
00:22:38,300 --> 00:22:39,750
and I do greedy denoising, right?

513
00:22:39,750 --> 00:22:41,500
So I'm denoising, and I'm going to predict

514
00:22:41,500 --> 00:22:44,060
the most likely noiseless--

515
00:22:44,060 --> 00:22:46,820
less noisy image, and then I'll do that iteratively.

516
00:22:46,820 --> 00:22:51,420
Will I get the most likely estimate of the noiseless image

517
00:22:51,420 --> 00:22:54,020
given whatever I condition on?

518
00:22:54,020 --> 00:22:57,020
What do you think?

519
00:22:57,020 --> 00:22:59,440
Anyone want to give a guess?

520
00:23:03,160 --> 00:23:04,508
It's a little tricky.

521
00:23:04,508 --> 00:23:06,800
OK, so it's the same exact thing with diffusion models.

522
00:23:06,800 --> 00:23:09,500
So diffusion models are also iterative step by step.

523
00:23:09,500 --> 00:23:11,060
In every sampling step, you're going

524
00:23:11,060 --> 00:23:14,560
to try to remove a little bit of noise but not all the noise.

525
00:23:14,560 --> 00:23:19,585
And if you do that and you make your prediction

526
00:23:19,585 --> 00:23:20,960
and it's going to try to maximize

527
00:23:20,960 --> 00:23:24,160
the conditional probability of slightly less noisy given

528
00:23:24,160 --> 00:23:29,080
slightly more noisy, than that won't necessarily

529
00:23:29,080 --> 00:23:33,080
result in a sequence of samples that- a sequence of denoising

530
00:23:33,080 --> 00:23:36,280
steps that maximizes the probability

531
00:23:36,280 --> 00:23:39,800
at the end of the sequence for the same kind of reason.

532
00:23:39,800 --> 00:23:42,743
But with VAEs, it's not really sequential.

533
00:23:42,743 --> 00:23:43,660
There's just one step.

534
00:23:43,660 --> 00:23:47,280
There's just encode, decode.

535
00:23:47,280 --> 00:23:49,192
So I think with VAEs, if I'm actually

536
00:23:49,192 --> 00:23:50,900
having a VAE that models a full sentence,

537
00:23:50,900 --> 00:23:53,760
there's no sequential process where my sampling could

538
00:23:53,760 --> 00:23:57,240
be deviating from the most likely sample

539
00:23:57,240 --> 00:23:58,860
for the entire joint configuration.

540
00:23:58,860 --> 00:24:04,440
OK, something more for you to think about after the lecture.

541
00:24:04,440 --> 00:24:05,770
But I will say one thing.

542
00:24:05,770 --> 00:24:07,520
I mentioned the diffusion models because I

543
00:24:07,520 --> 00:24:10,000
think there's not as much work in trying

544
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:12,482
to do intelligent inference through diffusion models.

545
00:24:12,482 --> 00:24:14,940
There's a lot of work on doing intelligent inference that's

546
00:24:14,940 --> 00:24:17,180
better than greedy sampling for language models that

547
00:24:17,180 --> 00:24:19,640
are autoregressive, but less for diffusion models.

548
00:24:19,640 --> 00:24:22,080
So maybe an interesting open topic for you to work on.

549
00:24:22,080 --> 00:24:24,765
How do you take higher likelihood samples

550
00:24:24,765 --> 00:24:26,140
from a diffusion model than you'd

551
00:24:26,140 --> 00:24:30,740
get with the regular denoising process?

552
00:24:30,740 --> 00:24:35,060
I'm sure there's some work on that, but just not as much.

553
00:24:35,060 --> 00:24:38,180
So now let's look at some fancier inference

554
00:24:38,180 --> 00:24:44,580
strategies than just doing autoregressive greedy sampling.

555
00:24:44,580 --> 00:24:46,540
Also, remember that in most language models,

556
00:24:46,540 --> 00:24:49,320
you just take random samples.

557
00:24:49,320 --> 00:24:52,520
You predict a distribution over the next token,

558
00:24:52,520 --> 00:24:55,020
and then you randomly sample from that distribution.

559
00:24:55,020 --> 00:24:57,300
And certainly, a random sample is not necessarily

560
00:24:57,300 --> 00:24:59,220
going to be the most likely sample.

561
00:24:59,220 --> 00:25:00,980
So if I want to answer a question like,

562
00:25:00,980 --> 00:25:04,260
what is the caption of this molecule,

563
00:25:04,260 --> 00:25:07,260
I might get a random description that is low probability just

564
00:25:07,260 --> 00:25:08,140
by chance.

565
00:25:08,140 --> 00:25:10,610
That's why we want to do better inference to get higher

566
00:25:10,610 --> 00:25:13,050
probability samples so we can answer questions

567
00:25:13,050 --> 00:25:14,030
more accurately.

568
00:25:16,750 --> 00:25:21,390
So the Best-of-N approach is really simple.

569
00:25:21,390 --> 00:25:25,330
It just says, go with your random sampling,

570
00:25:25,330 --> 00:25:28,010
and do it N times.

571
00:25:28,010 --> 00:25:31,890
Take N samples from your autoregressive models,

572
00:25:31,890 --> 00:25:35,650
independently and identically, and then

573
00:25:35,650 --> 00:25:38,890
evaluate the total likelihood of each sequence

574
00:25:38,890 --> 00:25:39,790
that you generated.

575
00:25:39,790 --> 00:25:41,630
So remember, when you're sampling these sequences,

576
00:25:41,630 --> 00:25:43,710
you're not sampling the max likelihood sequence.

577
00:25:43,710 --> 00:25:46,450
You're sampling a random sequence from the distribution.

578
00:25:46,450 --> 00:25:49,210
But you can evaluate the likelihood of any sequence using

579
00:25:49,210 --> 00:25:49,910
the chain rule.

580
00:25:49,910 --> 00:25:53,690
So I can evaluate the probability of whatever sequence

581
00:25:53,690 --> 00:25:55,570
I generated by just multiplying all

582
00:25:55,570 --> 00:25:59,410
the conditional probabilities under my model together.

583
00:25:59,410 --> 00:26:02,010
So here, I sample one sequence, a stimulant that

584
00:26:02,010 --> 00:26:03,850
enhances cognitive function.

585
00:26:03,850 --> 00:26:06,422
And then I run that through my autoregressive model

586
00:26:06,422 --> 00:26:08,630
by just multiplying all the conditional probabilities

587
00:26:08,630 --> 00:26:10,390
together, and I get 0.7.

588
00:26:10,390 --> 00:26:11,810
And then I sample another time.

589
00:26:11,810 --> 00:26:13,230
This time, I got a different random sample

590
00:26:13,230 --> 00:26:14,870
a stimulate that improves thinking.

591
00:26:14,870 --> 00:26:17,750
When you play with a language model, type in the same

592
00:26:17,750 --> 00:26:18,317
prompt twice.

593
00:26:18,317 --> 00:26:20,150
You'll get two different answers, typically,

594
00:26:20,150 --> 00:26:23,550
because it's sampling from a distribution.

595
00:26:23,550 --> 00:26:24,610
OK, here's another one.

596
00:26:24,610 --> 00:26:25,950
And then maybe I'll say--

597
00:26:25,950 --> 00:26:28,892
I'll do this N times, and I will say,

598
00:26:28,892 --> 00:26:30,850
this is the one that maximizes the probability.

599
00:26:30,850 --> 00:26:33,550
So this is the most likely caption in my--

600
00:26:33,550 --> 00:26:36,590
according to my model or my data distribution.

601
00:26:36,590 --> 00:26:37,390
Yeah.

602
00:26:37,390 --> 00:26:41,670
AUDIENCE: At each time step, when you sample the next token

603
00:26:41,670 --> 00:26:44,310
in the output sequence, is it with proportion

604
00:26:44,310 --> 00:26:46,250
to the probability distribution?

605
00:26:46,250 --> 00:26:48,010
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

606
00:26:48,010 --> 00:26:50,550
So the way that autoregressive sampling usually

607
00:26:50,550 --> 00:26:53,855
works is you output a categorical distribution

608
00:26:53,855 --> 00:26:55,730
over the next token, given the previous ones.

609
00:26:55,730 --> 00:26:57,063
That's each of the conditionals.

610
00:26:57,063 --> 00:26:59,390
And then you just take a sample from that distribution.

611
00:26:59,390 --> 00:27:01,182
There's a parameter called the temperature,

612
00:27:01,182 --> 00:27:05,820
where you can scale how flat or spiky the distribution is.

613
00:27:05,820 --> 00:27:09,320
And so if you set the temperature to 1,

614
00:27:09,320 --> 00:27:11,100
then you're taking unbiased samples

615
00:27:11,100 --> 00:27:12,983
from the data distribution.

616
00:27:12,983 --> 00:27:14,900
But sometimes you want to scale it differently

617
00:27:14,900 --> 00:27:17,760
in order to get mode-seeking behavior,

618
00:27:17,760 --> 00:27:20,080
like you just want to take the likelihood samples.

619
00:27:20,080 --> 00:27:20,620
Yeah.

620
00:27:20,620 --> 00:27:22,120
AUDIENCE: Does this mean that you're

621
00:27:22,120 --> 00:27:24,580
going to try with, say, all possible combinations of

622
00:27:24,580 --> 00:27:25,220
[INAUDIBLE]?

623
00:27:25,220 --> 00:27:28,220
Because I guess it's going to be very computationally expensive.

624
00:27:28,220 --> 00:27:29,720
PHILLIP ISOLA: Right, good question.

625
00:27:29,720 --> 00:27:32,700
So are you to try with all possible combinations?

626
00:27:32,700 --> 00:27:34,720
You can make that argument and say, well,

627
00:27:34,720 --> 00:27:37,180
if I did try with all possible combinations,

628
00:27:37,180 --> 00:27:39,040
then this would work.

629
00:27:39,040 --> 00:27:41,100
Like, I could find, truly, the max likelihood

630
00:27:41,100 --> 00:27:43,260
sample under my model.

631
00:27:43,260 --> 00:27:45,320
But that would be incredibly expensive.

632
00:27:45,320 --> 00:27:48,340
So instead, you only-- you sample from the model,

633
00:27:48,340 --> 00:27:51,760
and maybe you turn the temperature down a little bit.

634
00:27:51,760 --> 00:27:55,820
So you take-- it's trying to take high-probability samples.

635
00:27:55,820 --> 00:27:59,220
But if you turn it down to 0, then it's

636
00:27:59,220 --> 00:28:01,540
going to take the greedy sample, and that might not

637
00:28:01,540 --> 00:28:02,560
maximize probability.

638
00:28:02,560 --> 00:28:04,855
So you want it to be a temperature that's non-zero,

639
00:28:04,855 --> 00:28:06,480
but not like infinity, where you'd just

640
00:28:06,480 --> 00:28:09,840
be searching over the space of all possible random sequences.

641
00:28:09,840 --> 00:28:10,520
Yeah.

642
00:28:10,520 --> 00:28:12,440
AUDIENCE: Would it make sense to think of a heuristic

643
00:28:12,440 --> 00:28:14,898
where this is your starting point, but then you improve it?

644
00:28:14,898 --> 00:28:17,143
For example, you have to take the 0.8,

645
00:28:17,143 --> 00:28:18,560
and you create a small improvement

646
00:28:18,560 --> 00:28:20,800
where you take chemical out, you put stimulant in,

647
00:28:20,800 --> 00:28:22,232
and then maybe now you get 0.9.

648
00:28:22,232 --> 00:28:23,940
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, that's a great idea.

649
00:28:23,940 --> 00:28:27,080
So could you take this solution and then say, what

650
00:28:27,080 --> 00:28:28,680
if I change one of the words?

651
00:28:28,680 --> 00:28:30,380
Will I get a better solution?

652
00:28:30,380 --> 00:28:34,920
So in general, this is the whole space of optimization

653
00:28:34,920 --> 00:28:36,020
and statistical inference.

654
00:28:36,020 --> 00:28:38,213
Like, you could do MCMC, where you start to--

655
00:28:38,213 --> 00:28:39,380
you could do Gibbs sampling.

656
00:28:39,380 --> 00:28:41,213
You could do all kinds of interesting things

657
00:28:41,213 --> 00:28:43,480
where you take your current solution

658
00:28:43,480 --> 00:28:47,660
and you try to just modify one dimension of it at a time,

659
00:28:47,660 --> 00:28:49,920
or you try to do hill climbing, where you just

660
00:28:49,920 --> 00:28:52,100
look for a local perturbation that improves it.

661
00:28:52,100 --> 00:28:54,240
So there's just a very rich space

662
00:28:54,240 --> 00:28:57,060
of methods that can try to optimize over that sequence

663
00:28:57,060 --> 00:28:57,780
to improve it.

664
00:28:57,780 --> 00:28:59,840
You could take a language model and say, hey, rewrite this

665
00:28:59,840 --> 00:29:01,298
in 10 different ways that you think

666
00:29:01,298 --> 00:29:02,590
might improve the performance.

667
00:29:02,590 --> 00:29:04,550
That's another thing that people are doing.

668
00:29:04,550 --> 00:29:07,170
OK.

669
00:29:07,170 --> 00:29:11,770
But Best-of-N-- interestingly enough, to my knowledge,

670
00:29:11,770 --> 00:29:14,610
this Best-of-N sampling is about as good as anything right now.

671
00:29:14,610 --> 00:29:16,770
And it's like most naive--

672
00:29:16,770 --> 00:29:18,450
It's rejection sampling, essentially.

673
00:29:18,450 --> 00:29:23,630
Or it's just, sample 10 independent things or N

674
00:29:23,630 --> 00:29:25,790
independent things, and take the best one,

675
00:29:25,790 --> 00:29:27,590
and reject all the other ones.

676
00:29:27,590 --> 00:29:29,573
But there's smarter sampling methods.

677
00:29:29,573 --> 00:29:31,990
And one of the other sampling methods that's quite popular

678
00:29:31,990 --> 00:29:32,985
is called beam search.

679
00:29:32,985 --> 00:29:34,610
So this is, like, a tree search method.

680
00:29:34,610 --> 00:29:36,870
It's a little bit like what you were suggesting.

681
00:29:36,870 --> 00:29:40,390
So here, what you do is at every step

682
00:29:40,390 --> 00:29:42,510
of your autoregressive generation,

683
00:29:42,510 --> 00:29:49,010
you maintain a memory of the top-k best next words,

684
00:29:49,010 --> 00:29:54,670
and you start expanding out not just the best

685
00:29:54,670 --> 00:29:58,030
sample or a single sample, but more than one sample at a time.

686
00:29:58,030 --> 00:30:00,690
So maybe I'll say, well, strong or mild

687
00:30:00,690 --> 00:30:02,190
are both equally probable.

688
00:30:02,190 --> 00:30:03,667
And then given strong, then there's

689
00:30:03,667 --> 00:30:06,250
these two other possibilities that are the highest probability

690
00:30:06,250 --> 00:30:07,310
possibilities.

691
00:30:07,310 --> 00:30:10,192
And I'll expand this tree out with some branching factor k.

692
00:30:10,192 --> 00:30:11,650
And then at the end of the day I'll

693
00:30:11,650 --> 00:30:15,810
just look at the path along that tree that has max probability.

694
00:30:15,810 --> 00:30:19,250
So a little bit of-- it's like a tree search method,

695
00:30:19,250 --> 00:30:21,690
as opposed to just like an independent random search

696
00:30:21,690 --> 00:30:27,290
method, but otherwise it's the same idea.

697
00:30:27,290 --> 00:30:31,770
So again, there's a lot of other fancy search methods

698
00:30:31,770 --> 00:30:33,930
that you could use.

699
00:30:33,930 --> 00:30:35,490
Best-of-N seems to be the simplest

700
00:30:35,490 --> 00:30:37,870
and also works about as well as anything,

701
00:30:37,870 --> 00:30:39,730
as far as I can tell right now.

702
00:30:39,730 --> 00:30:41,910
And beam search is maybe the most popular,

703
00:30:41,910 --> 00:30:43,583
slightly more advanced method.

704
00:30:43,583 --> 00:30:46,250
So in language models, you might have an option like beam search

705
00:30:46,250 --> 00:30:48,935
on or off, so you can try playing with that.

706
00:30:48,935 --> 00:30:50,310
Now, what about Chain-of-Thought?

707
00:30:50,310 --> 00:30:51,685
We talked about chain of thought,

708
00:30:51,685 --> 00:30:56,250
or Jacob Andreas, in his talk, talked about chain of thought.

709
00:30:56,250 --> 00:31:01,200
Chain-of-Thought is-- I'll refer to it as an emergent

710
00:31:01,200 --> 00:31:03,600
or learned search.

711
00:31:03,600 --> 00:31:05,460
So here's an example.

712
00:31:05,460 --> 00:31:09,020
This is just from ChatGPT last night.

713
00:31:09,020 --> 00:31:12,120
So I want you to use beam search to solve the following problem.

714
00:31:12,120 --> 00:31:13,740
I could just use beam search.

715
00:31:13,740 --> 00:31:16,740
I could say, what is 4 times 5 plus 10?

716
00:31:16,740 --> 00:31:17,840
That's the problem.

717
00:31:17,840 --> 00:31:20,440
I could put that through my LLM, expand out

718
00:31:20,440 --> 00:31:23,900
all the possible sequences of completions,

719
00:31:23,900 --> 00:31:26,680
and then take the one that has highest probability, and that

720
00:31:26,680 --> 00:31:29,320
might be a better solution.

721
00:31:29,320 --> 00:31:30,760
As long as probability is related

722
00:31:30,760 --> 00:31:34,260
to actual accuracy on math, which it might or might not be,

723
00:31:34,260 --> 00:31:36,480
that would be a better solution.

724
00:31:36,480 --> 00:31:41,000
But instead, I'll just say, maybe if I prompt you,

725
00:31:41,000 --> 00:31:44,200
you can do that beam search yourself

726
00:31:44,200 --> 00:31:47,000
because you've observed, in your training corpus,

727
00:31:47,000 --> 00:31:51,870
examples of this kind of behavior.

728
00:31:51,870 --> 00:31:54,120
Or maybe you've been tuned with reinforcement learning

729
00:31:54,120 --> 00:31:55,180
and other methods.

730
00:31:55,180 --> 00:31:56,572
But basically, it does work.

731
00:31:56,572 --> 00:31:57,530
So here's what it does.

732
00:31:57,530 --> 00:32:00,810
It output a whole bunch of preamble that I'm going to skip.

733
00:32:00,810 --> 00:32:02,390
And then it says, OK, here's my beam

734
00:32:02,390 --> 00:32:04,650
search of two different sampling trajectories.

735
00:32:04,650 --> 00:32:07,390
And it turns out I think the better one is the 4

736
00:32:07,390 --> 00:32:09,350
times 5 plus 10 equals 30.

737
00:32:09,350 --> 00:32:11,090
So it could go 4 times 5 plus 10.

738
00:32:11,090 --> 00:32:13,630
And then I say, my next sequence of tokens

739
00:32:13,630 --> 00:32:16,530
will be- I'll parse that as 20 plus 10,

740
00:32:16,530 --> 00:32:19,110
and then finally, I'll get to 30.

741
00:32:19,110 --> 00:32:20,450
So what is Chain-of-Thought?

742
00:32:20,450 --> 00:32:24,150
Chain-of-Thought is like doing a reasoning or search process that

743
00:32:24,150 --> 00:32:26,590
could be written with a classical algorithm,

744
00:32:26,590 --> 00:32:29,390
but it's the LLM doing it itself and writing

745
00:32:29,390 --> 00:32:35,690
the intermediate steps into the text space, into its context,

746
00:32:35,690 --> 00:32:38,590
and then autoregressively continuing on.

747
00:32:38,590 --> 00:32:41,270
So one way of thinking about Chain-of-Thought.

748
00:32:45,750 --> 00:32:51,830
So that was all about how to get higher likelihood sequences

749
00:32:51,830 --> 00:32:54,890
from an autoregressive model by doing some kind of search

750
00:32:54,890 --> 00:32:57,970
or optimization over the space of possible sequences

751
00:32:57,970 --> 00:33:00,010
that are somewhat likely under the model.

752
00:33:00,010 --> 00:33:02,510
So you're being guided by the model, the model's likelihood,

753
00:33:02,510 --> 00:33:05,490
but then you're trying to find even more likely configurations.

754
00:33:05,490 --> 00:33:07,550
But it turns out you can do the same exact thing,

755
00:33:07,550 --> 00:33:11,490
but not for likelihood, but for any kind of scoring function.

756
00:33:11,490 --> 00:33:14,490
So I could say, what if my scoring function is

757
00:33:14,490 --> 00:33:18,218
a measure of how fun or whatever fun-ness?

758
00:33:18,218 --> 00:33:19,510
So this caption's not very fun.

759
00:33:19,510 --> 00:33:20,850
It sounds kind of scientific.

760
00:33:20,850 --> 00:33:22,610
OK, here's one.

761
00:33:22,610 --> 00:33:25,610
So what is-- this molecule is nature's tiny cheerleader,

762
00:33:25,610 --> 00:33:27,970
jumping into your bloodstream with pompoms.

763
00:33:27,970 --> 00:33:29,570
The language model gave me that.

764
00:33:29,570 --> 00:33:31,070
I didn't come up with that.

765
00:33:31,070 --> 00:33:32,850
Anyway, that's more fun.

766
00:33:32,850 --> 00:33:35,350
And then maybe it will output emojis, and that's the best.

767
00:33:35,350 --> 00:33:37,550
And so now what I can do is I can sample Best-of-N,

768
00:33:37,550 --> 00:33:40,290
but not according to likelihood or a learned model but something

769
00:33:40,290 --> 00:33:41,150
else.

770
00:33:41,150 --> 00:33:42,790
Maybe this is people online.

771
00:33:42,790 --> 00:33:44,490
This is people voting online.

772
00:33:44,490 --> 00:33:45,890
Maybe it's humans.

773
00:33:45,890 --> 00:33:47,270
And then I'll say--

774
00:33:47,270 --> 00:33:48,430
OK, I'll take that one.

775
00:33:51,815 --> 00:33:53,940
And this is a really popular thing to do right now.

776
00:33:53,940 --> 00:33:56,080
This is the basis of what's called reinforcement learning

777
00:33:56,080 --> 00:33:58,440
from human feedback, where we give humans-- rate all

778
00:33:58,440 --> 00:34:00,315
the sequences, and then you pick the best one

779
00:34:00,315 --> 00:34:02,040
that the humans rated.

780
00:34:02,040 --> 00:34:04,380
And you could change it to any function.

781
00:34:04,380 --> 00:34:05,540
Here's scientific accuracy.

782
00:34:05,540 --> 00:34:06,623
It's a different function.

783
00:34:06,623 --> 00:34:09,000
Maybe I downloaded a model of scientific accuracy,

784
00:34:09,000 --> 00:34:14,000
and now it picks this as the best description.

785
00:34:14,000 --> 00:34:14,560
Yeah.

786
00:34:14,560 --> 00:34:16,480
AUDIENCE: So in this as your score,

787
00:34:16,480 --> 00:34:18,719
is that another model that you're training?

788
00:34:18,719 --> 00:34:20,739
PHILLIP ISOLA: It can be absolutely anything.

789
00:34:20,739 --> 00:34:23,560
So something that takes in a sentence and gives you a

790
00:34:23,560 --> 00:34:24,880
scalar out.

791
00:34:24,880 --> 00:34:26,940
It could be another model that you've trained.

792
00:34:26,940 --> 00:34:28,540
It could be a human brain.

793
00:34:28,540 --> 00:34:29,940
It could be an animal brain.

794
00:34:29,940 --> 00:34:32,340
It could be a formal proof checker.

795
00:34:32,340 --> 00:34:34,480
I'll show you another example.

796
00:34:34,480 --> 00:34:36,560
This is a really popular type of scoring function

797
00:34:36,560 --> 00:34:38,199
called verification.

798
00:34:38,199 --> 00:34:41,400
Verification usually has the connotation

799
00:34:41,400 --> 00:34:45,960
that you're going to actually check that the output is correct

800
00:34:45,960 --> 00:34:47,737
using some formal process.

801
00:34:47,737 --> 00:34:49,320
In this case, the formal process might

802
00:34:49,320 --> 00:34:53,179
be a compiler or a mathematical proof checker,

803
00:34:53,179 --> 00:34:55,000
or maybe the verifier here.

804
00:34:55,000 --> 00:34:57,220
So here, we have- the input is, write

805
00:34:57,220 --> 00:35:00,260
a Python program to compute the Fibonacci sequence.

806
00:35:00,260 --> 00:35:02,360
The language model outputs two possibilities.

807
00:35:02,360 --> 00:35:04,100
It could output infinite possibilities,

808
00:35:04,100 --> 00:35:05,553
but I'm just showing two.

809
00:35:05,553 --> 00:35:07,220
And then the verifier could be something

810
00:35:07,220 --> 00:35:10,333
that has- it knows what the Fibonacci sequence is.

811
00:35:10,333 --> 00:35:11,000
So it can check.

812
00:35:11,000 --> 00:35:13,540
It can run the program and see if the program matches

813
00:35:13,540 --> 00:35:15,940
the Fibonacci sequence.

814
00:35:15,940 --> 00:35:18,540
So the verifier, in this case-- if I have the Fibonacci

815
00:35:18,540 --> 00:35:21,480
sequence, I can check that the output of the LLM matches.

816
00:35:21,480 --> 00:35:24,900
And if it does, then I say that that's my solution,

817
00:35:24,900 --> 00:35:27,940
and I can just do Best-of-N in this fashion.

818
00:35:27,940 --> 00:35:29,293
And why is this a good idea?

819
00:35:29,293 --> 00:35:31,460
Fundamentally, it's a good idea because verification

820
00:35:31,460 --> 00:35:33,500
is easier than generation.

821
00:35:33,500 --> 00:35:35,860
So checking that I correctly solved a math problem

822
00:35:35,860 --> 00:35:39,100
or I correctly made code that has some desirable property

823
00:35:39,100 --> 00:35:41,100
is just fundamentally easier than coming up

824
00:35:41,100 --> 00:35:42,720
with the code that has that property,

825
00:35:42,720 --> 00:35:45,700
or coming up with the solution to the math problem.

826
00:35:45,700 --> 00:35:48,800
So proving a theorem is fundamentally harder

827
00:35:48,800 --> 00:35:51,580
than checking that a proof is correct.

828
00:35:51,580 --> 00:35:57,840
This goes back to things like t the class

829
00:35:57,840 --> 00:36:01,180
of problems that these are checkable in polynomial time,

830
00:36:01,180 --> 00:36:03,200
but we don't know if-- and we expect

831
00:36:03,200 --> 00:36:04,920
that they don't have a way of solving

832
00:36:04,920 --> 00:36:08,642
the problem in polynomial time, as long as P does not equal NP.

833
00:36:08,642 --> 00:36:10,100
So verification can be really easy.

834
00:36:10,100 --> 00:36:14,780
You just check that you actually output the Fibonacci sequence.

835
00:36:14,780 --> 00:36:16,820
But how did I write an algorithm that does that?

836
00:36:16,820 --> 00:36:17,860
It might not be trivial.

837
00:36:20,800 --> 00:36:25,240
Another example of this would be verifying that--

838
00:36:25,240 --> 00:36:27,240
doing prime factorization, coming up

839
00:36:27,240 --> 00:36:29,520
with-- finding the prime factors for some big number

840
00:36:29,520 --> 00:36:34,280
is a hard problem, but checking that some set of factors

841
00:36:34,280 --> 00:36:36,740
multiply to equal the number is an easy problem.

842
00:36:39,600 --> 00:36:45,000
So verification can be the scoring method.

843
00:36:45,000 --> 00:36:46,910
So you can do all these same methods,

844
00:36:46,910 --> 00:36:50,090
not just for language models and autoregressive models,

845
00:36:50,090 --> 00:36:52,230
but also for other types of generative models.

