and here's how it worked.

1040
00:45:31,910 --> 00:45:35,610
So basically, you had a generative model,

1041
00:45:35,610 --> 00:45:39,890
and you had a predictor of how memorable an image is.

1042
00:45:39,890 --> 00:45:42,620
And you had a manipulation, a search process

1043
00:45:42,620 --> 00:45:44,370
that you would do over the latent variable

1044
00:45:44,370 --> 00:45:46,730
space of that generative model.

1045
00:45:46,730 --> 00:45:49,665
And now, what you can do is you can just-- actually,

1046
00:45:49,665 --> 00:45:50,790
this is all differentiable.

1047
00:45:50,790 --> 00:45:53,490
So this type of search process is, in some ways,

1048
00:45:53,490 --> 00:45:55,578
easier than with autoregressive language models.

1049
00:45:55,578 --> 00:45:57,870
In language models, you have to have these tree search,

1050
00:45:57,870 --> 00:45:59,970
these symbolic search methods.

1051
00:45:59,970 --> 00:46:03,910
In this pixel search problem, everything is differentiable.

1052
00:46:03,910 --> 00:46:06,390
So you just can do gradient descent to do the search.

1053
00:46:06,390 --> 00:46:07,890
So you can just backprop all the way

1054
00:46:07,890 --> 00:46:10,010
to the manipulation of the latent variables that

1055
00:46:10,010 --> 00:46:13,273
will maximize the objective of being more memorable.

1056
00:46:13,273 --> 00:46:14,690
OK, so here's this dog, and here's

1057
00:46:14,690 --> 00:46:18,650
what it looks like when you make it more or less memorable.

1058
00:46:18,650 --> 00:46:21,630
So you zoom in, and the eyes get really big.

1059
00:46:21,630 --> 00:46:23,330
That's making it more memorable.

1060
00:46:23,330 --> 00:46:25,050
You can actually measure that, and people

1061
00:46:25,050 --> 00:46:27,760
do remember that photo better.

1062
00:46:27,760 --> 00:46:29,700
And then if you make it less memorable,

1063
00:46:29,700 --> 00:46:33,140
it will become grainy and hard to interpret and small.

1064
00:46:36,960 --> 00:46:39,900
So we presented that as a probe into human perception.

1065
00:46:39,900 --> 00:46:43,260
But this type of method has become more popular as--

1066
00:46:45,920 --> 00:46:47,625
under names like reinforcement learning

1067
00:46:47,625 --> 00:46:49,000
from human feedback, where you're

1068
00:46:49,000 --> 00:46:54,400
trying to make language models output data that humans--

1069
00:46:54,400 --> 00:46:57,520
a model of a human ends up saying is good data

1070
00:46:57,520 --> 00:47:00,420
and is preferable or aesthetic or meaningful

1071
00:47:00,420 --> 00:47:03,200
or non-toxic or fair or whatever.

1072
00:47:03,200 --> 00:47:04,540
So here's a few more examples.

1073
00:47:04,540 --> 00:47:08,880
So you can also optimize in the latent space to maximize a model

1074
00:47:08,880 --> 00:47:11,520
of aesthetic preference-- that's in the middle--

1075
00:47:11,520 --> 00:47:15,080
or to optimize the model of emotional--

1076
00:47:15,080 --> 00:47:16,360
sorry.

1077
00:47:16,360 --> 00:47:19,080
In the middle row is aesthetics, and on the bottom

1078
00:47:19,080 --> 00:47:20,343
is emotional valence.

1079
00:47:20,343 --> 00:47:22,510
So each of these concepts is a little bit different,

1080
00:47:22,510 --> 00:47:24,140
and you can visualize them in this way.

1081
00:47:24,140 --> 00:47:28,780
So simple symmetric structures, big,

1082
00:47:28,780 --> 00:47:31,080
centered objects are easy to remember.

1083
00:47:31,080 --> 00:47:34,020
So this is the most memorable version of that snake.

1084
00:47:34,020 --> 00:47:36,060
But the most aesthetic version of the snake

1085
00:47:36,060 --> 00:47:38,520
is actually more like-- it has this depth-of-field effect.

1086
00:47:38,520 --> 00:47:40,140
It's a little bit hard to see.

1087
00:47:40,140 --> 00:47:42,058
It's at a 3/4 angle.

1088
00:47:42,058 --> 00:47:44,600
The most aesthetic landscape is going to be really saturated,

1089
00:47:44,600 --> 00:47:47,220
but the most memorable landscape will make everything big.

1090
00:47:47,220 --> 00:47:49,980
People remember things when they're big.

1091
00:47:49,980 --> 00:47:52,340
The most aesthetic dog is really cute,

1092
00:47:52,340 --> 00:47:55,042
but doesn't have this big head, but the big head and big eyes

1093
00:47:55,042 --> 00:47:56,000
make it more memorable.

1094
00:47:56,000 --> 00:47:58,060
So you can see interesting differences.

1095
00:47:58,060 --> 00:47:59,500
You're just optimizing-- searching

1096
00:47:59,500 --> 00:48:01,580
through the space of generative possibilities

1097
00:48:01,580 --> 00:48:05,460
to find the one that maximizes the score function.

1098
00:48:05,460 --> 00:48:06,140
Yeah.

1099
00:48:06,140 --> 00:48:08,060
AUDIENCE: Is the significance from this result

1100
00:48:08,060 --> 00:48:11,763
really-- it's of decoding how our brain works already.

1101
00:48:11,763 --> 00:48:13,180
PHILLIP ISOLA: That was the intent

1102
00:48:13,180 --> 00:48:15,940
of the paper was it was more like a psychology paper.

1103
00:48:15,940 --> 00:48:17,620
But I'm trying to connect it here

1104
00:48:17,620 --> 00:48:21,200
to the trend of just optimized human preferences.

1105
00:48:21,200 --> 00:48:22,910
But yeah, that's exactly the intent.

1106
00:48:22,910 --> 00:48:25,050
Yep.

1107
00:48:25,050 --> 00:48:28,250
AUDIENCE: I wonder how much, in the research,

1108
00:48:28,250 --> 00:48:34,210
if the representation of the objects in an image versus how

1109
00:48:34,210 --> 00:48:36,490
the image looks was evaluated in terms

1110
00:48:36,490 --> 00:48:39,450
of how the model understands whether the aesthetic

1111
00:48:39,450 --> 00:48:41,170
or the emotional balance is connected

1112
00:48:41,170 --> 00:48:44,410
with the dog or the layout of the picture.

1113
00:48:44,410 --> 00:48:45,090
Yeah.

1114
00:48:45,090 --> 00:48:49,490
I wonder if there were any takeaways in that aspect.

1115
00:48:49,490 --> 00:48:51,870
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, I would say, look up the paper.

1116
00:48:51,870 --> 00:48:55,430
It's called GANalyze-- it has--

1117
00:48:55,430 --> 00:48:57,930
GANalyze is the keyword.

1118
00:48:57,930 --> 00:49:02,130
So what factors determine aesthetics or memorability?

1119
00:49:02,130 --> 00:49:04,490
Is it layout, or is it the central object,

1120
00:49:04,490 --> 00:49:07,910
or is it some of the other properties, the color?

1121
00:49:07,910 --> 00:49:08,930
Is it shape?

1122
00:49:08,930 --> 00:49:10,670
We have some analysis of that.

1123
00:49:10,670 --> 00:49:14,010
But generally, I would say it's--

1124
00:49:14,010 --> 00:49:16,433
the main object matters the most, and being centered.

1125
00:49:16,433 --> 00:49:17,350
So layout does matter.

1126
00:49:17,350 --> 00:49:20,010
Being centered helps a lot, and being close up helps a lot.

1127
00:49:20,010 --> 00:49:21,940
AUDIENCE: And in the loss function,

1128
00:49:21,940 --> 00:49:24,200
there wasn't some sort of--

1129
00:49:24,200 --> 00:49:25,880
there wasn't some of characterization

1130
00:49:25,880 --> 00:49:29,840
of where the object that we're interested in is in the image.

1131
00:49:29,840 --> 00:49:32,020
It was just [INAUDIBLE] that was there.

1132
00:49:32,020 --> 00:49:34,000
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, so the loss function

1133
00:49:34,000 --> 00:49:37,880
is simply a neural net that is trained to predict--

1134
00:49:37,880 --> 00:49:41,980
to regress how likely a human is to remember this image,

1135
00:49:41,980 --> 00:49:45,660
and it's fit to human data.

1136
00:49:45,660 --> 00:49:46,160
OK.

1137
00:49:46,160 --> 00:49:47,243
Yeah, question over there.

1138
00:49:47,243 --> 00:49:49,980
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] wondering about the perception.

1139
00:49:49,980 --> 00:49:53,000
So when we're running things where it's subjective to human

1140
00:49:53,000 --> 00:49:55,540
perception-- so this one-- memorability, aesthetics,

1141
00:49:55,540 --> 00:49:57,120
we're measuring something--

1142
00:49:57,120 --> 00:49:58,840
to what extent do we need to account

1143
00:49:58,840 --> 00:50:02,440
for cultural differences or demographic, age differences?

1144
00:50:02,440 --> 00:50:04,020
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

1145
00:50:04,020 --> 00:50:08,800
So to what extent is this dependent on culture, age,

1146
00:50:08,800 --> 00:50:10,440
individual characteristics?

1147
00:50:10,440 --> 00:50:14,420
So in psychology, there's this term, individual differences,

1148
00:50:14,420 --> 00:50:16,180
which is about all of those factors.

1149
00:50:16,180 --> 00:50:17,760
It's like there's one set of factors

1150
00:50:17,760 --> 00:50:19,640
that are common and shared across all humans,

1151
00:50:19,640 --> 00:50:21,740
and there's other factors-- you model humans

1152
00:50:21,740 --> 00:50:25,220
as being a common peace and a peace that's individual to them.

1153
00:50:25,220 --> 00:50:27,940
And this is only looking at the common piece,

1154
00:50:27,940 --> 00:50:31,800
and we didn't do any controls on culture or age or background.

1155
00:50:31,800 --> 00:50:35,380
I think there are statistics on the participant

1156
00:50:35,380 --> 00:50:38,025
demographics in the paper, but we never made any comparison.

1157
00:50:38,025 --> 00:50:39,400
That would be really interesting.

1158
00:50:39,400 --> 00:50:41,500
But oftentimes, what you might want to do

1159
00:50:41,500 --> 00:50:43,620
is find the common thing that characterizes

1160
00:50:43,620 --> 00:50:46,180
a general population first, and then-- and then

1161
00:50:46,180 --> 00:50:49,980
the second-order effect is maybe younger people

1162
00:50:49,980 --> 00:50:51,860
like this more than that, and older people

1163
00:50:51,860 --> 00:50:53,920
like that more than this, and it's a secondary effect.

1164
00:50:53,920 --> 00:50:56,337
So I think that would be a really interesting future work.

1165
00:50:58,800 --> 00:51:03,540
So that was a little bit of a tangent

1166
00:51:03,540 --> 00:51:08,300
into memorability and aesthetics.

1167
00:51:08,300 --> 00:51:11,748
But the point is that if you have a really good generative

1168
00:51:11,748 --> 00:51:14,040
model, then you don't have to just use its raw outputs.

1169
00:51:14,040 --> 00:51:16,400
You can actually optimize and search

1170
00:51:16,400 --> 00:51:20,840
and take different samples and do multiple rollouts

1171
00:51:20,840 --> 00:51:25,600
and change the latent variables to maximize

1172
00:51:25,600 --> 00:51:27,600
some function of interest, either the likelihood

1173
00:51:27,600 --> 00:51:29,840
or a different scoring function.

1174
00:51:29,840 --> 00:51:34,200
Well, there's also a new paradigm to do all of this

1175
00:51:34,200 --> 00:51:39,200
stuff, which is what I think Eric Jang nicely described

1176
00:51:39,200 --> 00:51:41,460
in this blog post as the "just ask" paradigm.

1177
00:51:41,460 --> 00:51:42,762
It's the prompting paradigm.

1178
00:51:42,762 --> 00:51:44,220
It's the Chain-of-Thought paradigm.

1179
00:51:44,220 --> 00:51:46,240
Rather than doing beam search, you can say--

1180
00:51:46,240 --> 00:51:48,305
just ask the model to do beam search.

1181
00:51:48,305 --> 00:51:49,180
Don't actually do it.

1182
00:51:49,180 --> 00:51:51,820
Just say, do beam search, or, let's think step by step.

1183
00:51:51,820 --> 00:51:54,680
Now it's going to do some kind of sequential reasoning process,

1184
00:51:54,680 --> 00:51:57,200
instead of having to hard code some sequential reasoning

1185
00:51:57,200 --> 00:51:58,160
process.

1186
00:51:58,160 --> 00:52:01,480
So Chain-of-Thought is, just ask for symbolic search

1187
00:52:01,480 --> 00:52:04,760
over reasoning that's written in language.

1188
00:52:04,760 --> 00:52:06,100
And can we do the same?

1189
00:52:06,100 --> 00:52:08,340
Just ask for this continuous reward optimization.

1190
00:52:08,340 --> 00:52:10,780
I have some aesthetics reward function.

1191
00:52:10,780 --> 00:52:14,150
Can I just say, give me an image that's aesthetic level 10,

1192
00:52:14,150 --> 00:52:16,370
aesthetic level 11?

1193
00:52:16,370 --> 00:52:18,490
So interestingly enough, this works.

1194
00:52:18,490 --> 00:52:22,670
So here was just another fun example from a few years ago.

1195
00:52:22,670 --> 00:52:25,800
So here is a prompt to DALL-E 1, I believe,

1196
00:52:25,800 --> 00:52:28,050
which is a painting of a mountain next to a waterfall.

1197
00:52:28,050 --> 00:52:30,630
You get some OK paintings.

1198
00:52:30,630 --> 00:52:33,707
But what if I say, a painting of a beautiful mountain--

1199
00:52:33,707 --> 00:52:36,040
or a beautiful painting of a mountain next to waterfall?

1200
00:52:36,040 --> 00:52:37,415
It's a little bit more saturated.

1201
00:52:37,415 --> 00:52:38,623
It's a little more beautiful.

1202
00:52:38,623 --> 00:52:40,270
It's like I steered it in the direction

1203
00:52:40,270 --> 00:52:42,228
that the model has interpreted as being related

1204
00:52:42,228 --> 00:52:43,710
to beauty and aesthetics.

1205
00:52:43,710 --> 00:52:46,670
What if I say, a very beautiful painting of a mountain

1206
00:52:46,670 --> 00:52:48,150
next to a waterfall?

1207
00:52:48,150 --> 00:52:49,950
It's getting a little better.

1208
00:52:49,950 --> 00:52:51,870
What if I say a, very, very--

1209
00:52:51,870 --> 00:52:54,750
now, two units of reward?

1210
00:52:54,750 --> 00:52:56,310
A little better still.

1211
00:52:56,310 --> 00:52:57,982
OK.

1212
00:52:57,982 --> 00:52:59,690
How many times can I do it, do you think?

1213
00:52:59,690 --> 00:53:03,710
What about, very, very, very, very, very, very, very?

1214
00:53:03,710 --> 00:53:06,270
It's definitely more beautiful, right?

1215
00:53:06,270 --> 00:53:09,350
So then you can go extreme.

1216
00:53:09,350 --> 00:53:11,750
I think it maxed out around there.

1217
00:53:11,750 --> 00:53:16,180
So these models did learn this conditional distribution

1218
00:53:16,180 --> 00:53:20,380
of conditioned on the reward I want to achieve, what

1219
00:53:20,380 --> 00:53:22,280
should the output look like?

1220
00:53:22,280 --> 00:53:24,620
I don't actually have to necessarily search explicitly

1221
00:53:24,620 --> 00:53:27,720
for reward-maximizing images.

1222
00:53:27,720 --> 00:53:30,620
I can just tell it, conditioned on this reward,

1223
00:53:30,620 --> 00:53:33,625
you should make a painting that matches that reward.

1224
00:53:33,625 --> 00:53:36,000
So people have shown this in a lot of different contexts.

1225
00:53:36,000 --> 00:53:38,980
Now, here's another fun one from Jon Barron.

1226
00:53:38,980 --> 00:53:40,880
He says, this is a Level 2 Reuben.

1227
00:53:40,880 --> 00:53:46,500
And now he says, make me a level 1 to the--

1228
00:53:46,500 --> 00:53:49,100
1e15 Reuben.

1229
00:53:49,100 --> 00:53:51,280
So you can check out his these things.

1230
00:53:51,280 --> 00:53:53,960
There was another trend a few years ago called, make it more.

1231
00:53:53,960 --> 00:53:55,740
You just take an image, and you say, make it more X;

1232
00:53:55,740 --> 00:53:58,080
make it more X; make it more X. You keep on doing it.

1233
00:53:58,080 --> 00:54:00,473
And eventually, it always devolves into this like picture

1234
00:54:00,473 --> 00:54:01,140
of the universe.

1235
00:54:01,140 --> 00:54:05,420
That's the most-- this is a 1e40 Reuben.

1236
00:54:05,420 --> 00:54:09,000
And in this sequence of tweets, there's more.

1237
00:54:09,000 --> 00:54:12,380
There's level 2, 5, 10, and so forth.

1238
00:54:12,380 --> 00:54:14,200
And it does scale.

1239
00:54:14,200 --> 00:54:16,080
But it's not perfect.

1240
00:54:16,080 --> 00:54:18,540
These emergent abilities, these learned abilities--

1241
00:54:18,540 --> 00:54:21,040
they're not like the abilities that you

1242
00:54:21,040 --> 00:54:23,940
get by doing well-understood search algorithms

1243
00:54:23,940 --> 00:54:26,600
because they're just going to depend on what the model-- how

1244
00:54:26,600 --> 00:54:29,260
the model learned from the data to do that type of search.

1245
00:54:29,260 --> 00:54:31,160
So it's going to depend on the data distribution on which it

1246
00:54:31,160 --> 00:54:31,620
was trained.

1247
00:54:31,620 --> 00:54:32,480
It's not going to be something we

1248
00:54:32,480 --> 00:54:34,120
have guarantees or proofs for.

1249
00:54:34,120 --> 00:54:36,750
So here, we say 1e negative 40 Reuben,

1250
00:54:36,750 --> 00:54:38,663
and it doesn't make a tiny, tiny Reuben.

1251
00:54:38,663 --> 00:54:40,080
It, instead, makes this microscope

1252
00:54:40,080 --> 00:54:41,780
because it has that connotation.

1253
00:54:41,780 --> 00:54:44,720
That's what the data told it to do.

1254
00:54:44,720 --> 00:54:47,240
OK.

1255
00:54:47,240 --> 00:54:48,680
Yeah, question.

1256
00:54:48,680 --> 00:54:50,760
AUDIENCE: So understand this for images--

1257
00:54:50,760 --> 00:54:53,640
on the first slide, you had just prompt for--

1258
00:54:53,640 --> 00:54:55,620
in the text form, for a Chain-of-Thought

1259
00:54:55,620 --> 00:54:58,480
and like beam search, I think.

1260
00:54:58,480 --> 00:55:02,680
Do you explicitly have to code that into text models

1261
00:55:02,680 --> 00:55:05,400
if you want to implement that kind of search

1262
00:55:05,400 --> 00:55:08,460
at the end because of the autoregressive nature of it?

1263
00:55:10,165 --> 00:55:11,540
PHILLIP ISOLA: Do you mean do you

1264
00:55:11,540 --> 00:55:13,878
have to have the language model trained

1265
00:55:13,878 --> 00:55:15,420
in a way that anticipates that you'll

1266
00:55:15,420 --> 00:55:16,880
be using it with beam search?

1267
00:55:16,880 --> 00:55:18,140
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

1268
00:55:18,140 --> 00:55:20,182
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, that could potentially help.

1269
00:55:20,182 --> 00:55:22,660
I think a lot of search algorithms are meant to be very

1270
00:55:22,660 --> 00:55:26,140
broad-use, that whatever--

1271
00:55:26,140 --> 00:55:28,060
any autoregressive model will be improved

1272
00:55:28,060 --> 00:55:31,580
by this generic, Best-of-N search approach.

1273
00:55:31,580 --> 00:55:32,700
It's very safe.

1274
00:55:32,700 --> 00:55:35,260
But if you have some search approach which is more like,

1275
00:55:35,260 --> 00:55:36,193
my search will be--

1276
00:55:36,193 --> 00:55:38,360
I'll prompt the system to, let's think step by step,

1277
00:55:38,360 --> 00:55:40,040
then that will depend a lot on how it was trained,

1278
00:55:40,040 --> 00:55:41,832
and you want to couple those two processes.

1279
00:55:41,832 --> 00:55:45,100
You want to make sure that the language model was trained

1280
00:55:45,100 --> 00:55:47,860
on data where whenever it said, let's think step by step,

1281
00:55:47,860 --> 00:55:50,620
it had a really good reasoning process.

1282
00:55:50,620 --> 00:55:53,780
And that was an empirical discovery that that works well,

1283
00:55:53,780 --> 00:55:55,620
but you could also design your data

1284
00:55:55,620 --> 00:55:58,380
set in conjunction with the search algorithm

1285
00:55:58,380 --> 00:55:59,540
if you wanted to.

1286
00:55:59,540 --> 00:56:02,780
We'll talk a little bit about that at the end.

1287
00:56:02,780 --> 00:56:04,680
So that was all about search.

1288
00:56:04,680 --> 00:56:07,330
Now let's go into inference methods

1289
00:56:07,330 --> 00:56:10,610
that actually change the model's behavior,

1290
00:56:10,610 --> 00:56:14,370
maybe update its weights, maybe do something else.

1291
00:56:14,370 --> 00:56:19,330
And then they update the model's behavior in such a way

1292
00:56:19,330 --> 00:56:22,755
that over time, during the test sequence-- maybe

1293
00:56:22,755 --> 00:56:24,880
I have a self-driving car, and it's driving around,

1294
00:56:24,880 --> 00:56:26,050
and it's getting smarter and smarter

1295
00:56:26,050 --> 00:56:27,390
as it drives around the city.

1296
00:56:27,390 --> 00:56:30,702
So it's actually a different model at the end of the trip

1297
00:56:30,702 --> 00:56:31,910
than it was at the beginning.

1298
00:56:31,910 --> 00:56:33,743
It's a smarter model at the end of the trip.

1299
00:56:33,743 --> 00:56:35,070
It's learned something new.

1300
00:56:35,070 --> 00:56:36,833
So there's a lot of methods that do this.

1301
00:56:36,833 --> 00:56:38,750
The first I'll mention is in-context learning,

1302
00:56:38,750 --> 00:56:40,530
but this is just a reminder.

1303
00:56:40,530 --> 00:56:42,930
So in-context learning is--

1304
00:56:42,930 --> 00:56:45,830
I have a data sequence that I'm processing,

1305
00:56:45,830 --> 00:56:48,550
and the sequence includes examples of desirable behavior.

1306
00:56:48,550 --> 00:56:52,690
So the sequence is a supervised learning sequence.

1307
00:56:52,690 --> 00:56:56,210
Input x, output y, another input x, another output y.

1308
00:56:56,210 --> 00:56:58,870
So x1, y1, x2, y2, and so forth.

1309
00:56:58,870 --> 00:57:01,930
And then at the very end of the sequence I have x query,

1310
00:57:01,930 --> 00:57:05,430
and I have-- and the language model, the autoregressive model

1311
00:57:05,430 --> 00:57:07,985
should learn to complete that sequence.

1312
00:57:07,985 --> 00:57:09,610
And what would be a natural completion?

1313
00:57:09,610 --> 00:57:11,050
It would be to follow the pattern.

1314
00:57:11,050 --> 00:57:14,150
And what is the name of following a pattern of x,

1315
00:57:14,150 --> 00:57:15,530
y example pairs?

1316
00:57:15,530 --> 00:57:17,470
The name of that is supervised learning.

1317
00:57:17,470 --> 00:57:19,990
So in-context learning is just the definition

1318
00:57:19,990 --> 00:57:22,090
of supervised learning.

1319
00:57:22,090 --> 00:57:24,310
It's learning to do supervised learning.

1320
00:57:24,310 --> 00:57:27,190
It's a transformer that is-- or an autoregressive language

1321
00:57:27,190 --> 00:57:30,525
model, in this case, that's learning to do supervised

1322
00:57:30,525 --> 00:57:32,150
learning because supervised learning is

1323
00:57:32,150 --> 00:57:35,230
the problem of taking a sequence of x, y examples followed

1324
00:57:35,230 --> 00:57:38,130
by a query and outputting the answer to the query.

1325
00:57:40,750 --> 00:57:44,110
So we saw that before.

1326
00:57:44,110 --> 00:57:48,910
So I want to now talk about a slightly different way

1327
00:57:48,910 --> 00:57:52,790
of doing that, which is called test-time training.

1328
00:57:52,790 --> 00:57:54,465
So there's this really interesting paper

1329
00:57:54,465 --> 00:57:56,590
from a few years ago that tried to understand, what

1330
00:57:56,590 --> 00:57:59,590
is in-context learning doing?

1331
00:57:59,590 --> 00:58:02,070
Is in-context learning a learning algorithm like the ones

1332
00:58:02,070 --> 00:58:06,620
we know, like gradient descent on a supervised regression loss?

1333
00:58:06,620 --> 00:58:10,460
Or is in-context learning something fundamentally new?

1334
00:58:10,460 --> 00:58:13,240
And they set things up like this.

1335
00:58:13,240 --> 00:58:15,900
They said, let's let f parameterized by theta

1336
00:58:15,900 --> 00:58:19,620
be a transformer; x is a query; and c is the context.

1337
00:58:19,620 --> 00:58:23,380
So the context is going to be the in-context examples, x, y,

1338
00:58:23,380 --> 00:58:25,360
demonstrations, and then we have a new query.

1339
00:58:25,360 --> 00:58:29,860
We want to complete it based on the patterns in the context.

1340
00:58:29,860 --> 00:58:33,825
So we're going to rewrite f of x comma context.

1341
00:58:33,825 --> 00:58:35,700
That's like-- can you concatenate the context

1342
00:58:35,700 --> 00:58:36,360
with the query?

1343
00:58:36,360 --> 00:58:38,040
That's the input to the language model.

1344
00:58:38,040 --> 00:58:40,900
We're going to rewrite that as another function, f

1345
00:58:40,900 --> 00:58:43,935
of x, with parameters theta prime

1346
00:58:43,935 --> 00:58:46,560
And we're going to try to see if these two things can be equal.

1347
00:58:49,300 --> 00:58:52,760
So what's the relationship between theta and theta prime?

1348
00:58:52,760 --> 00:58:55,900
There's some way of taking all the information

1349
00:58:55,900 --> 00:58:59,300
in the context and the attention mechanism

1350
00:58:59,300 --> 00:59:02,530
and getting rid of all of that, but somehow baking it, instead,

1351
00:59:02,530 --> 00:59:05,308
into a set of weights that has the same effect.

1352
00:59:05,308 --> 00:59:07,350
Let's say that the transformer is infinitely big.

1353
00:59:07,350 --> 00:59:08,630
These are universal approximators.

1354
00:59:08,630 --> 00:59:10,047
So this approximator can certainly

1355
00:59:10,047 --> 00:59:11,450
approximate that approximator.

1356
00:59:11,450 --> 00:59:14,250
This is a universal approximator, right?

1357
00:59:14,250 --> 00:59:17,220
But what's the relationship between theta and theta prime?

1358
00:59:17,220 --> 00:59:18,970
And the really interesting thing that they

1359
00:59:18,970 --> 00:59:20,890
showed is that there are cases where

1360
00:59:20,890 --> 00:59:23,650
the relationship between theta and theta prime is theta prime

1361
00:59:23,650 --> 00:59:26,050
is gradient descent on theta with respect

1362
00:59:26,050 --> 00:59:29,210
to a particular loss function.

1363
00:59:29,210 --> 00:59:34,250
So I take my theta from my original transformer.

1364
00:59:34,250 --> 00:59:37,890
I then do gradient descent to update the parameters

1365
00:59:37,890 --> 00:59:41,830
according to some loss, which is dependent on the context.

1366
00:59:41,830 --> 00:59:44,570
The context is part of that loss.

1367
00:59:44,570 --> 00:59:48,090
And I will get a new transformer that, when not showing

1368
00:59:48,090 --> 00:59:51,810
any context at all, just via the weights,

1369
00:59:51,810 --> 00:59:55,090
it will output the same result as the original transformer

1370
00:59:55,090 --> 00:59:56,337
with that context.

1371
00:59:56,337 --> 00:59:58,670
So what they're saying is that this original transformer

1372
00:59:58,670 --> 01:00:00,878
with this context is doing something

1373
01:00:00,878 --> 01:00:02,670
like what you would have gotten if you just

1374
01:00:02,670 --> 01:00:04,950
did gradient descent on that context with respect

1375
01:00:04,950 --> 01:00:07,330
to the weights.

1376
01:00:07,330 --> 01:00:08,330
It's a little confusing.

1377
01:00:08,330 --> 01:00:09,450
Does it make sense?

1378
01:00:13,670 --> 01:00:15,910
So in certain cases--

1379
01:00:15,910 --> 01:00:20,000
they showed this for one layer, linear self-attention.

1380
01:00:20,000 --> 01:00:21,750
It's not the full picture, but they showed

1381
01:00:21,750 --> 01:00:23,870
this for a simple toy case.

1382
01:00:23,870 --> 01:00:26,950
And in certain cases, like that one that they showed,

1383
01:00:26,950 --> 01:00:29,830
you can express in-context learning

1384
01:00:29,830 --> 01:00:32,190
as gradient descent over the context examples

1385
01:00:32,190 --> 01:00:34,025
to update the final mapping from the query

1386
01:00:34,025 --> 01:00:36,150
to the answer, where that mapping is only specified

1387
01:00:36,150 --> 01:00:40,670
by the weights and biases of the transformer, not by the context.

1388
01:00:40,670 --> 01:00:41,670
Yeah.

1389
01:00:41,670 --> 01:00:43,390
AUDIENCE: Is the argument something

1390
01:00:43,390 --> 01:00:48,030
to the effect of showing that the activations

1391
01:00:48,030 --> 01:00:51,870
within the transformer, after having processed the context,

1392
01:00:51,870 --> 01:00:55,590
could be computed as the original with gradient descent

1393
01:00:55,590 --> 01:00:56,090
step?

1394
01:00:56,090 --> 01:00:57,770
PHILLIP ISOLA: Exactly, yeah.

1395
01:00:57,770 --> 01:00:59,710
So I didn't dig too much into this paper.

1396
01:00:59,710 --> 01:01:03,030
I don't remember if it's purely an empirical argument that they

1397
01:01:03,030 --> 01:01:04,530
showed this, or they actually proved

1398
01:01:04,530 --> 01:01:07,410
the cases under which it works, but you can do either one.

1399
01:01:07,410 --> 01:01:09,030
I think that if they proved something,

1400
01:01:09,030 --> 01:01:11,150
it's for a linear case and something very simple.

1401
01:01:11,150 --> 01:01:14,835
But there's a lot of follow-up papers to this, too.

1402
01:01:14,835 --> 01:01:16,210
AUDIENCE: I just remember this is

1403
01:01:16,210 --> 01:01:19,150
very similar to a paper I read.

1404
01:01:19,150 --> 01:01:22,170
And I think it's in some inverse reinforcement learning, when

1405
01:01:22,170 --> 01:01:24,757
you give a human correct interventions,

1406
01:01:24,757 --> 01:01:27,090
and you assume that those interventions are intentional,

1407
01:01:27,090 --> 01:01:29,370
and you want to update your weights based

1408
01:01:29,370 --> 01:01:30,270
on that intervention.

1409
01:01:30,270 --> 01:01:31,635
They use a similar method.

1410
01:01:31,635 --> 01:01:33,010
And they don't take the gradient.

1411
01:01:33,010 --> 01:01:34,970
They just do a difference between trajectories

1412
01:01:34,970 --> 01:01:36,970
and the feature counts.

1413
01:01:36,970 --> 01:01:40,090
But I was wondering if--

1414
01:01:40,090 --> 01:01:44,090
is this the same idea or--

1415
01:01:44,090 --> 01:01:44,970
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

1416
01:01:44,970 --> 01:01:47,387
OK, so the question is, in inverse reinforcement learning,

1417
01:01:47,387 --> 01:01:51,290
there might be similar methods where you have implicitly

1418
01:01:51,290 --> 01:01:55,407
defined functions defined by the reward.

1419
01:01:55,407 --> 01:01:57,740
I need to think more and parse that question a bit more,

1420
01:01:57,740 --> 01:01:59,400
but I would say, yes, I think there

1421
01:01:59,400 --> 01:02:01,020
should be connections to that.

1422
01:02:04,080 --> 01:02:04,580
OK.

1423
01:02:04,580 --> 01:02:09,300
So basically, this raises this very tantalizing question.

1424
01:02:09,300 --> 01:02:12,880
Well, if in-context learning is somehow related to or even,

1425
01:02:12,880 --> 01:02:14,800
in some cases, equivalent to gradient descent

1426
01:02:14,800 --> 01:02:17,600
over the context, why not just do gradient descent

1427
01:02:17,600 --> 01:02:18,740
over the context?

1428
01:02:18,740 --> 01:02:20,320
That might have some advantages.

1429
01:02:20,320 --> 01:02:22,028
So what are the advantages it might have?

1430
01:02:22,028 --> 01:02:23,960
Well, one is that in-context learning

1431
01:02:23,960 --> 01:02:26,662
is learned learning there's no guarantees on its behavior.

1432
01:02:26,662 --> 01:02:29,120
It's just going to behave in a way that depends on the data

1433
01:02:29,120 --> 01:02:31,080
distribution it was trained on.

1434
01:02:31,080 --> 01:02:34,342
So in-context learning might not converge.

1435
01:02:34,342 --> 01:02:36,800
It doesn't have these properties like gradient descent has.

1436
01:02:36,800 --> 01:02:38,967
Gradient descent, if I take small enough step sizes,

1437
01:02:38,967 --> 01:02:41,010
will always find a local minimum.

1438
01:02:41,010 --> 01:02:42,760
In-context learning-- there's no guarantee

1439
01:02:42,760 --> 01:02:44,572
that if I show it more and more context,

1440
01:02:44,572 --> 01:02:46,280
it will get better and better, or it will

1441
01:02:46,280 --> 01:02:48,012
find a local minimum at least.

1442
01:02:48,012 --> 01:02:49,220
So it's less well-understood.

1443
01:02:49,220 --> 01:02:51,040
It's something that is learned and we don't

1444
01:02:51,040 --> 01:02:52,820
have as much control over.

1445
01:02:52,820 --> 01:02:57,200
We only control it via the data and the learning process.

1446
01:02:57,200 --> 01:03:00,400
So if I can replace in-context learning with gradient descent,

1447
01:03:00,400 --> 01:03:03,060
then I might have an object that has better guarantees,

1448
01:03:03,060 --> 01:03:05,123
that I can understand better.

1449
01:03:05,123 --> 01:03:06,540
Additionally, gradient descent can

1450
01:03:06,540 --> 01:03:09,260
leverage more test-time compute than in-context learning

1451
01:03:09,260 --> 01:03:11,660
because in-context learning is just feed-forward

1452
01:03:11,660 --> 01:03:13,035
pass through the transformer.

1453
01:03:13,035 --> 01:03:15,660
But gradient dissent can be an iterative optimization algorithm

1454
01:03:15,660 --> 01:03:17,440
that does forward and backwards.

1455
01:03:17,440 --> 01:03:20,060
That's more compute, and it can do it many, many, many times.

1456
01:03:20,060 --> 01:03:21,820
So one thing you want to look out

1457
01:03:21,820 --> 01:03:23,902
for is how can you leverage compute?

1458
01:03:23,902 --> 01:03:25,860
The more compute you can leverage-- oftentimes,

1459
01:03:25,860 --> 01:03:27,600
there will be some scaling law with that.

1460
01:03:27,600 --> 01:03:29,380
So gradient descent has this possibility

1461
01:03:29,380 --> 01:03:33,142
of leveraging more compute at test time.

1462
01:03:33,142 --> 01:03:34,600
But there's possible disadvantages.

1463
01:03:34,600 --> 01:03:37,272
Gradient descent is not the smartest learning algorithm.

1464
01:03:37,272 --> 01:03:38,980
In-context learning could learn something

1465
01:03:38,980 --> 01:03:40,500
that takes better updates, better

1466
01:03:40,500 --> 01:03:42,200
steps than gradient descent.

1467
01:03:42,200 --> 01:03:44,760
So it could be better than gradient descent.

1468
01:03:44,760 --> 01:03:46,300
But the papers, the theory papers

1469
01:03:46,300 --> 01:03:47,842
suggest that maybe it's not that much

1470
01:03:47,842 --> 01:03:50,220
different than gradient descent, but that's still only

1471
01:03:50,220 --> 01:03:51,470
for simple toy models.

1472
01:03:51,470 --> 01:03:54,880
Maybe in huge language models, in-context learning

1473
01:03:54,880 --> 01:03:56,630
is actually smarter than gradient descent.

1474
01:03:56,630 --> 01:03:59,410
That could be a possibility.

1475
01:03:59,410 --> 01:04:00,370
Yeah.

1476
01:04:00,370 --> 01:04:03,110
AUDIENCE: You say doing gradient descent over the context.

1477
01:04:03,110 --> 01:04:09,250
Are you suggesting retraining the model

1478
01:04:09,250 --> 01:04:13,490
on input sequences, where you have this context included,

1479
01:04:13,490 --> 01:04:17,690
or are you talking about-- there's prompt optimization

1480
01:04:17,690 --> 01:04:20,850
papers where you have some prefix tokens,

1481
01:04:20,850 --> 01:04:24,530
and then you do gradient descent to optimize the prefix tokens

1482
01:04:24,530 --> 01:04:27,020
for your downstream?

1483
01:04:27,020 --> 01:04:28,770
PHILLIP ISOLA: So the question is, are you

1484
01:04:28,770 --> 01:04:33,950
doing gradient descent on f, x, comma c or f, x, I think,

1485
01:04:33,950 --> 01:04:34,450
essentially?

1486
01:04:34,450 --> 01:04:37,510
I mean, there's a few ways you could do it, but in this case,

1487
01:04:37,510 --> 01:04:42,930
I'm suggesting gradient descent on queries without context

1488
01:04:42,930 --> 01:04:45,870
and trying to use the context examples just

1489
01:04:45,870 --> 01:04:48,370
to improve the mapping from the query directly to the answer

1490
01:04:48,370 --> 01:04:51,590
without the context.

1491
01:04:51,590 --> 01:04:53,250
But the other ideas are good, too.

1492
01:04:53,250 --> 01:04:54,070
Yeah.

1493
01:04:54,070 --> 01:04:54,690
Question.

1494
01:04:54,690 --> 01:04:57,790
AUDIENCE: Oh, yeah, I was thinking intuitively

1495
01:04:57,790 --> 01:05:01,150
about this other assertion of gradient descent

1496
01:05:01,150 --> 01:05:02,413
over in context learning.

1497
01:05:02,413 --> 01:05:04,830
And is it not fair to say that there's a limit to how much

1498
01:05:04,830 --> 01:05:07,150
you could train the model, just like an expert can

1499
01:05:07,150 --> 01:05:08,890
do a PhD in multiple fields?

1500
01:05:08,890 --> 01:05:12,550
If you do it in one and you gain the tools to develop

1501
01:05:12,550 --> 01:05:15,570
logical reasoning and capabilities,

1502
01:05:15,570 --> 01:05:19,030
then inference time might be the more adequate place

1503
01:05:19,030 --> 01:05:20,310
to do learning at.

1504
01:05:20,310 --> 01:05:23,870
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, so I think the question is,

1505
01:05:23,870 --> 01:05:26,390
if you do learning at training time,

1506
01:05:26,390 --> 01:05:28,390
then you have to be a generalist.

1507
01:05:28,390 --> 01:05:29,990
And maybe there's an infinite number

1508
01:05:29,990 --> 01:05:31,690
of scenarios you might come across,

1509
01:05:31,690 --> 01:05:34,450
and how can you learn to do well in all of them?

1510
01:05:34,450 --> 01:05:37,590
But if you do training at test time, when

1511
01:05:37,590 --> 01:05:41,610
you have a context of very related examples you're given,

1512
01:05:41,610 --> 01:05:43,857
then you, on the fly, learn a specialized skill,

1513
01:05:43,857 --> 01:05:46,190
and you don't have to have memorized the infinite number

1514
01:05:46,190 --> 01:05:47,460
of things you need to do.

1515
01:05:47,460 --> 01:05:50,380
And I think that's exactly why people like in-context learning

1516
01:05:50,380 --> 01:05:52,220
and test-time compute.

1517
01:05:52,220 --> 01:05:54,300
That's a good point.

1518
01:05:54,300 --> 01:05:57,760
OK, so anyway, the name for this is test-time training,

1519
01:05:57,760 --> 01:06:00,980
or that's one of the names that's emerged for this thing.

1520
01:06:00,980 --> 01:06:02,580
And the idea, just roughly, is you're

1521
01:06:02,580 --> 01:06:04,288
going to update the weights of your model

1522
01:06:04,288 --> 01:06:07,260
as a function of the test query, or maybe the entire test set.

1523
01:06:07,260 --> 01:06:10,900
You might have a sequence of test queries.

1524
01:06:10,900 --> 01:06:13,800
The test query could include these in-context examples.

1525
01:06:13,800 --> 01:06:15,840
And you update on the basis of those.

1526
01:06:15,840 --> 01:06:17,692
You train on those.

1527
01:06:17,692 --> 01:06:19,900
But there's also another, more extreme scenario where

1528
01:06:19,900 --> 01:06:23,500
the test query is just an x.

1529
01:06:23,500 --> 01:06:26,157
You don't get supervised in-context examples.

1530
01:06:26,157 --> 01:06:27,740
So can you still do test-time training

1531
01:06:27,740 --> 01:06:30,360
when I just get a single photo at test time,

1532
01:06:30,360 --> 01:06:34,060
or maybe a movie at test time, or maybe a book at test time?

1533
01:06:34,060 --> 01:06:35,320
It turns out you can.

1534
01:06:35,320 --> 01:06:36,520
I just have an image.

1535
01:06:36,520 --> 01:06:38,045
I don't have an image and labels.

1536
01:06:38,045 --> 01:06:38,920
I just have an image.

1537
01:06:38,920 --> 01:06:41,380
Can I improve my performance at predicting

1538
01:06:41,380 --> 01:06:42,700
the label of an image?

1539
01:06:42,700 --> 01:06:46,800
So it's not really in-context supervised learning anymore,

1540
01:06:46,800 --> 01:06:49,380
but you can do self-supervised test-time training.

1541
01:06:49,380 --> 01:06:53,360
And this paper from Sun and folks did that,

1542
01:06:53,360 --> 01:06:55,000
and it's really cool.

1543
01:06:55,000 --> 01:06:57,760
So what they did is they said, I have a system that's

1544
01:06:57,760 --> 01:06:59,940
going to try to label a photo.

1545
01:06:59,940 --> 01:07:03,440
Here's x, and it's going to try to predict the label, bird.

1546
01:07:03,440 --> 01:07:06,400
And I can decompose that system into a first trunk

1547
01:07:06,400 --> 01:07:10,120
of the network and then a task-specific head.

1548
01:07:10,120 --> 01:07:12,580
And it turns out that trunk of the network

1549
01:07:12,580 --> 01:07:17,920
I can train with a self-supervised head as well.

1550
01:07:17,920 --> 01:07:23,000
So I'm going to update the weights of f in order

1551
01:07:23,000 --> 01:07:26,720
to make them do better at solving a self-supervised task

1552
01:07:26,720 --> 01:07:29,140
like masked autoencoding.

1553
01:07:29,140 --> 01:07:31,360
So this is an MA, a masked autoencoder.

1554
01:07:31,360 --> 01:07:35,000
I remove some patches, and I try to predict the missing patches.

1555
01:07:35,000 --> 01:07:38,252
I will do that to learn-- to fine-tune f.

1556
01:07:38,252 --> 01:07:39,960
I might have a pre-trained f, and now I'm

1557
01:07:39,960 --> 01:07:42,360
going to try to fine tune it on the test example

1558
01:07:42,360 --> 01:07:44,630
with a self-supervised loss.

1559
01:07:44,630 --> 01:07:49,110
And then I will simply have a better representation.

1560
01:07:49,110 --> 01:07:51,750
And this can actually improve your performance

1561
01:07:51,750 --> 01:07:55,090
on correctly identifying that this is a bird.

1562
01:07:57,730 --> 01:07:59,310
So test-time training can be done

1563
01:07:59,310 --> 01:08:01,850
with an unsupervised objective at test time.

1564
01:08:01,850 --> 01:08:03,550
And this is a very general-purpose thing

1565
01:08:03,550 --> 01:08:04,050
you can do.

1566
01:08:04,050 --> 01:08:06,410
You're just adapting to your test scenario.

1567
01:08:06,410 --> 01:08:08,910
It's exactly like the point that the student over here

1568
01:08:08,910 --> 01:08:11,803
was making, that you can't pre-train for everything.

1569
01:08:11,803 --> 01:08:12,970
So let's say you go to Mars.

1570
01:08:12,970 --> 01:08:15,310
You want to, on the fly, adapt your representation

1571
01:08:15,310 --> 01:08:18,600
to be more sensitive to red or, somehow,

1572
01:08:18,600 --> 01:08:20,350
the distribution of images looks different

1573
01:08:20,350 --> 01:08:21,450
and you don't have to have labels.

1574
01:08:21,450 --> 01:08:23,033
You can just adapt your representation

1575
01:08:23,033 --> 01:08:25,029
and get better performance.

1576
01:08:25,029 --> 01:08:25,830
Yeah.

1577
01:08:25,830 --> 01:08:27,747
AUDIENCE: So here, you're updating the weights

1578
01:08:27,747 --> 01:08:29,870
to f or g task?

1579
01:08:29,870 --> 01:08:32,390
PHILLIP ISOLA: You're updating the weights to f only.

1580
01:08:32,390 --> 01:08:34,229
And then g task--

1581
01:08:34,229 --> 01:08:37,029
I believe g task stays frozen, but maybe you

1582
01:08:37,029 --> 01:08:40,069
have to do some stitching operation.

1583
01:08:40,069 --> 01:08:43,569
AUDIENCE: How do you ensure you don't forget?

1584
01:08:43,569 --> 01:08:44,776
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

1585
01:08:44,776 --> 01:08:46,109
I'd have to look into the paper.

1586
01:08:46,109 --> 01:08:47,317
I don't remember the details.

1587
01:08:47,317 --> 01:08:50,630
But I think it's just if you don't change f that much,

1588
01:08:50,630 --> 01:08:52,290
you just perturb it slightly, there's

1589
01:08:52,290 --> 01:08:53,590
a regime in which it works.

1590
01:08:53,590 --> 01:08:57,250
If you had trained f with a really big learning rate

1591
01:08:57,250 --> 01:08:59,770
and changed the weights entirely, then no guarantees.

1592
01:08:59,770 --> 01:09:01,550
But they show cases in which it works.

1593
01:09:04,930 --> 01:09:07,010
So test-time training is really cool.

1594
01:09:07,010 --> 01:09:10,130
And here's a paper from about a week or two ago, which

1595
01:09:10,130 --> 01:09:14,470
is a really exciting new result. This is from folks at MIT,

1596
01:09:14,470 --> 01:09:16,569
so maybe some of them.

1597
01:09:16,569 --> 01:09:22,010
Jacob Andreas and Yoon Kim are the professors, and then

1598
01:09:22,010 --> 01:09:23,490
the students.

1599
01:09:23,490 --> 01:09:26,050
And what they showed is that there's

1600
01:09:26,050 --> 01:09:27,970
this challenge called the arc challenge, which

1601
01:09:27,970 --> 01:09:30,729
is supposed to be a really hard challenge for deep learning

1602
01:09:30,729 --> 01:09:32,290
to solve.

1603
01:09:32,290 --> 01:09:36,250
And the arc challenge is cases like this,

1604
01:09:36,250 --> 01:09:38,290
that you get in-context examples-- you

1605
01:09:38,290 --> 01:09:41,040
get a few examples of input and output,

1606
01:09:41,040 --> 01:09:42,840
and humans can look at this last one,

1607
01:09:42,840 --> 01:09:45,800
and you can answer what the pattern is.

1608
01:09:45,800 --> 01:09:47,680
It's not trivial, but if you think of it,

1609
01:09:47,680 --> 01:09:52,960
you can get 50% or maybe higher on this benchmark.

1610
01:09:52,960 --> 01:09:56,580
So expert humans can get 80%, 90%.

1611
01:09:59,400 --> 01:10:03,160
And this challenge is kind of meant

1612
01:10:03,160 --> 01:10:05,822
to fool the deep nets of a few years ago.

1613
01:10:05,822 --> 01:10:07,280
So the deep nets of a few years ago

1614
01:10:07,280 --> 01:10:10,520
didn't really do any search or test-time optimization.

1615
01:10:10,520 --> 01:10:12,640
They just learned a general-purpose rule

1616
01:10:12,640 --> 01:10:17,000
that would map from data in the training set to correct answers

1617
01:10:17,000 --> 01:10:19,413
in the training set.

1618
01:10:19,413 --> 01:10:21,080
And so people thought, well, we better--

1619
01:10:21,080 --> 01:10:23,260
to solve problems like this, we need to do symbolic reasoning.

1620
01:10:23,260 --> 01:10:24,380
We need to do search.

1621
01:10:24,380 --> 01:10:26,183
We need to add all these fancy methods.

1622
01:10:26,183 --> 01:10:27,600
And what they showed in this paper

1623
01:10:27,600 --> 01:10:30,320
is you can get pretty far by only doing

1624
01:10:30,320 --> 01:10:32,760
this test-time training method.

1625
01:10:32,760 --> 01:10:37,300
So you simply do the regular test-time training

1626
01:10:37,300 --> 01:10:40,620
with supervised examples, not self-supervised.

1627
01:10:40,620 --> 01:10:43,700
You pre-train a model to be generally good at this type

1628
01:10:43,700 --> 01:10:45,940
of pattern recognition problem.

1629
01:10:45,940 --> 01:10:49,725
And then you-- given the few in-context examples,

1630
01:10:49,725 --> 01:10:51,100
you update the model to do better

1631
01:10:51,100 --> 01:10:53,020
at those in-context examples, gradient descent

1632
01:10:53,020 --> 01:10:55,260
on these supervised examples.

1633
01:10:55,260 --> 01:10:58,060
And then you apply it to your test query,

1634
01:10:58,060 --> 01:11:00,500
and you get the right answer.

1635
01:11:00,500 --> 01:11:02,540
And if, instead, you do--

1636
01:11:02,540 --> 01:11:04,440
fine-tuning is a training time optimization,

1637
01:11:04,440 --> 01:11:06,960
so fine tuning is not on the in-context examples,

1638
01:11:06,960 --> 01:11:10,560
but it's on the training set of similar problems.

1639
01:11:10,560 --> 01:11:13,060
Then it doesn't work as well because the in-context examples

1640
01:11:13,060 --> 01:11:14,200
are the most relevant.

1641
01:11:14,200 --> 01:11:16,242
This is exactly about your special--

1642
01:11:16,242 --> 01:11:18,200
you can't learn to do everything at train time,

1643
01:11:18,200 --> 01:11:19,480
so instead, you wait until test time.

1644
01:11:19,480 --> 01:11:20,938
You see the most relevant examples,

1645
01:11:20,938 --> 01:11:22,700
and then you fit to those examples,

1646
01:11:22,700 --> 01:11:25,760
but you just fit to them with gradient descent.

1647
01:11:25,760 --> 01:11:27,540
So I thought this is really interesting

1648
01:11:27,540 --> 01:11:30,820
because this arc challenge says, AGI has stalled.

1649
01:11:30,820 --> 01:11:31,760
New ideas are needed.

1650
01:11:31,760 --> 01:11:33,560
But this paper is kind of saying, wait a second.

1651
01:11:33,560 --> 01:11:35,352
Maybe a little bit of a new idea is needed,

1652
01:11:35,352 --> 01:11:37,650
but actually, they got the highest performance

1653
01:11:37,650 --> 01:11:43,150
that's been reported so far, and they did it with old methods.

1654
01:11:43,150 --> 01:11:46,110
They did it with gradient descent supervised learning.

1655
01:11:46,110 --> 01:11:48,390
Now, it happens to be on the in-context examples.

1656
01:11:48,390 --> 01:11:50,515
That's a new idea.

1657
01:11:50,515 --> 01:11:52,390
And they have some other tricks in the paper.

1658
01:11:52,390 --> 01:11:55,510
But otherwise, it's fairly old methods.

1659
01:11:55,510 --> 01:11:56,430
And it's interesting.

1660
01:11:56,430 --> 01:11:57,930
It raises the question, to me, that,

1661
01:11:57,930 --> 01:12:01,510
do we really need fancy new symbolic reasoning methods?

1662
01:12:01,510 --> 01:12:04,570
Or maybe gradient descent is still the workhorse,

1663
01:12:04,570 --> 01:12:07,100
and is still-- even for inference time, gradient descent

1664
01:12:07,100 --> 01:12:08,850
is one of the best things that you can do?

1665
01:12:08,850 --> 01:12:09,530
Yeah.

1666
01:12:09,530 --> 01:12:09,830
AUDIENCE: Sorry.

1667
01:12:09,830 --> 01:12:11,913
I'm maybe just a little confused on what they did.

1668
01:12:11,913 --> 01:12:14,130
So did they just feed in the two examples,

1669
01:12:14,130 --> 01:12:18,710
like and then the next one and say, what should this be?

1670
01:12:18,710 --> 01:12:19,710
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, no.

1671
01:12:19,710 --> 01:12:22,490
So it's not like it's a transformer that

1672
01:12:22,490 --> 01:12:23,670
takes these in context.

1673
01:12:23,670 --> 01:12:24,870
That doesn't work as well.

1674
01:12:27,490 --> 01:12:30,450
This is the training set for a new phase of learning

1675
01:12:30,450 --> 01:12:32,090
with gradient descent.

1676
01:12:32,090 --> 01:12:35,640
So they're simply feeding in this single image,

1677
01:12:35,640 --> 01:12:37,640
looking at the output, penalizing the difference

1678
01:12:37,640 --> 01:12:40,312
from this single image, backpropping the loss

1679
01:12:40,312 --> 01:12:42,020
to update the parameters of that mapping,

1680
01:12:42,020 --> 01:12:43,603
and then doing it on this single image

1681
01:12:43,603 --> 01:12:47,760
and doing that some number of times.

1682
01:12:47,760 --> 01:12:48,640
Make sense?

1683
01:12:48,640 --> 01:12:51,560
They're just doing supervised learning.

1684
01:12:51,560 --> 01:12:53,640
There's no transformer that's processing

1685
01:12:53,640 --> 01:12:55,020
a context in this case.

1686
01:12:55,020 --> 01:12:55,720
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

1687
01:12:55,720 --> 01:12:57,860
Isn't that, I guess, in a sense, cheating,

1688
01:12:57,860 --> 01:13:00,353
like, looking at your test loss?

1689
01:13:00,353 --> 01:13:02,020
PHILLIP ISOLA: Well, that's not cheating

1690
01:13:02,020 --> 01:13:03,220
according to this challenge.

1691
01:13:03,220 --> 01:13:05,800
The name of the challenge is given a few examples

1692
01:13:05,800 --> 01:13:09,200
of a new pattern, apply that pattern

1693
01:13:09,200 --> 01:13:11,400
to a query, a test query.

1694
01:13:11,400 --> 01:13:14,480
So they don't have the answer to the test query.

1695
01:13:14,480 --> 01:13:17,600
For a human, this is exactly how the challenge is stated.

1696
01:13:17,600 --> 01:13:19,220
Here is example behavior.

1697
01:13:19,220 --> 01:13:21,440
What is the correct behavior on this new query?

1698
01:13:21,440 --> 01:13:23,780
And they don't get to see the answer on this new query.

1699
01:13:23,780 --> 01:13:26,460
They only got to see the answer on the examples.

1700
01:13:26,460 --> 01:13:28,860
So this is the definition of the problem.

1701
01:13:28,860 --> 01:13:29,620
It's not cheating.

1702
01:13:32,340 --> 01:13:34,900
OK.

1703
01:13:34,900 --> 01:13:36,340
Cool.

1704
01:13:36,340 --> 01:13:38,800
But humans can do 80%, 90%.

1705
01:13:38,800 --> 01:13:40,680
Maybe François Chollet, who made it,

1706
01:13:40,680 --> 01:13:42,900
can probably do 100% on this.

1707
01:13:42,900 --> 01:13:45,400
So 62%-- maybe we're not quite there yet,

1708
01:13:45,400 --> 01:13:49,180
but that's interesting that you can go pretty far.

1709
01:13:49,180 --> 01:13:50,960
OK, so for the final few minutes,

1710
01:13:50,960 --> 01:13:56,500
I want to talk about this really exciting, current way of doing

1711
01:13:56,500 --> 01:13:59,500
things, which is to use search and inference

1712
01:13:59,500 --> 01:14:03,360
and optimization at test time to come up with better solutions,

1713
01:14:03,360 --> 01:14:08,740
and then distill those solutions back into the pre-training

1714
01:14:08,740 --> 01:14:10,020
the base model itself.

1715
01:14:10,020 --> 01:14:15,900
So fit to those optimized solutions and then repeat.

1716
01:14:15,900 --> 01:14:18,700
So this is how you can use search to give better examples

1717
01:14:18,700 --> 01:14:20,220
to train on.

1718
01:14:20,220 --> 01:14:21,800
So it's really a simple idea.

1719
01:14:21,800 --> 01:14:22,520
It's an old idea.

1720
01:14:22,520 --> 01:14:24,720
It's been around since the beginning of AI,

1721
01:14:24,720 --> 01:14:26,660
I guess, in some way or another.

1722
01:14:26,660 --> 01:14:29,770
It is, OK, come up with multiple answers.

1723
01:14:29,770 --> 01:14:33,190
Now we have some way of doing Best-of-N or tree search

1724
01:14:33,190 --> 01:14:38,130
or some kind of optimization to find better answers than the LLM

1725
01:14:38,130 --> 01:14:42,530
output by default. And now I can simply take that better answer

1726
01:14:42,530 --> 01:14:44,610
and call it a training pair for supervised

1727
01:14:44,610 --> 01:14:47,210
learning to fit the LLM to directly output that better

1728
01:14:47,210 --> 01:14:48,210
answer.

1729
01:14:48,210 --> 01:14:50,850
So I'm just improving my performance

1730
01:14:50,850 --> 01:14:56,395
by my own model search, plus the search algorithm.

1731
01:14:56,395 --> 01:14:58,770
Sometimes people call these self-improvement or recursive

1732
01:14:58,770 --> 01:14:59,478
self-improvement.

1733
01:14:59,478 --> 01:15:04,090
It's just a model that is initially crummy.

1734
01:15:04,090 --> 01:15:08,210
It does some optimization or verification process to come up

1735
01:15:08,210 --> 01:15:09,150
with better solutions.

1736
01:15:09,150 --> 01:15:11,450
And then it trains on those better solutions

1737
01:15:11,450 --> 01:15:12,630
to be a better model.

1738
01:15:15,132 --> 01:15:17,090
So you might be wondering, well, wait a second.

1739
01:15:17,090 --> 01:15:18,950
The information came in through the verifier here.

1740
01:15:18,950 --> 01:15:20,930
And that is-- that's one of the powerful ways

1741
01:15:20,930 --> 01:15:22,387
that a model can improve.

1742
01:15:22,387 --> 01:15:24,470
And you might not want to call that, self-improve.

1743
01:15:24,470 --> 01:15:27,390
That's improving with an external verifier.

1744
01:15:27,390 --> 01:15:29,550
Sometimes people mix the terms and might still

1745
01:15:29,550 --> 01:15:31,150
call that self-improve.

1746
01:15:31,150 --> 01:15:35,810
But you could also have this verifier be a model itself.

1747
01:15:35,810 --> 01:15:38,370
Remember, it's easier to learn to verify than to generate.

1748
01:15:38,370 --> 01:15:40,910
So I can learn to verify easily and quickly

1749
01:15:40,910 --> 01:15:43,530
and then use the verifier to improve the generator,

1750
01:15:43,530 --> 01:15:48,190
and then it's purely self-improvement.

1751
01:15:48,190 --> 01:15:51,013
So you can also do it the other way around.

1752
01:15:51,013 --> 01:15:53,430
So you can use search to improve learning and use learning

1753
01:15:53,430 --> 01:15:54,530
to improve search.

1754
01:15:54,530 --> 01:15:56,548
And this is basically the fundamental concept

1755
01:15:56,548 --> 01:15:58,590
in reinforcement learning, which we didn't really

1756
01:15:58,590 --> 01:15:59,670
have time to cover.

1757
01:15:59,670 --> 01:16:05,310
But I'll just tell you the basic loop in reinforcement learning.

1758
01:16:05,310 --> 01:16:07,905
Let's say you're playing the video game, Mario.

1759
01:16:07,905 --> 01:16:09,530
So you have a few actions you can make.

1760
01:16:09,530 --> 01:16:11,830
You want to jump over that--

1761
01:16:11,830 --> 01:16:15,470
whatever that thing is, that-- yeah, that tunnel thing--

1762
01:16:15,470 --> 01:16:16,430
that pipe.

1763
01:16:16,430 --> 01:16:17,970
He wants to jump over, that pipe.

1764
01:16:17,970 --> 01:16:20,830
So you're going to search for random action sequences

1765
01:16:20,830 --> 01:16:24,470
until you find one that will get a high score in the game.

1766
01:16:24,470 --> 01:16:27,850
So jumping will turn out to get a high score.

1767
01:16:27,850 --> 01:16:29,770
Then you're going to take that--

1768
01:16:29,770 --> 01:16:30,550
you searched.

1769
01:16:30,550 --> 01:16:33,390
You found the good sequence, the good sentence.

1770
01:16:33,390 --> 01:16:35,250
Here's the good sequence of actions.

1771
01:16:35,250 --> 01:16:37,810
And now you're going to fit a neural network to directly

1772
01:16:37,810 --> 01:16:39,370
predict that sequence.

1773
01:16:39,370 --> 01:16:41,770
So now you're going to say, in that situation,

1774
01:16:41,770 --> 01:16:44,110
my search algorithm found that it was good to jump,

1775
01:16:44,110 --> 01:16:46,757
so I'll train a neural network to directly go from that--

1776
01:16:46,757 --> 01:16:48,590
I don't have to do a search process anymore.

1777
01:16:48,590 --> 01:16:49,950
Now I can just directly map.

1778
01:16:49,950 --> 01:16:52,200
It's called a policy that directly maps from the state

1779
01:16:52,200 --> 01:16:54,890
to the optimal action.

1780
01:16:54,890 --> 01:16:57,227
And then I'll repeat the process.

1781
01:16:57,227 --> 01:16:58,810
Now I'm going to repeat, and I'm going

1782
01:16:58,810 --> 01:17:01,090
to guide the search to be preferentially doing things

1783
01:17:01,090 --> 01:17:03,462
that the neural network thought were good.

1784
01:17:03,462 --> 01:17:05,670
And so I'm not going to search over random sequences,

1785
01:17:05,670 --> 01:17:08,850
but sequences that the model-- the policy already thinks

1786
01:17:08,850 --> 01:17:11,650
are good sequences.

1787
01:17:11,650 --> 01:17:15,450
And then that iteration just keeps on improving.

1788
01:17:15,450 --> 01:17:18,070
And if you have some score in the game,

1789
01:17:18,070 --> 01:17:20,410
like a reward function in reinforcement learning,

1790
01:17:20,410 --> 01:17:25,080
then this will provably get to maximal reward

1791
01:17:25,080 --> 01:17:26,180
under some conditions.

1792
01:17:29,775 --> 01:17:32,150
So that's the name of the game in reinforcement learning.

1793
01:17:32,150 --> 01:17:32,380
Yep

1794
01:17:32,380 --> 01:17:33,755
AUDIENCE: I had a quick question.

1795
01:17:33,755 --> 01:17:36,600
These examples that are reinforcement learning

1796
01:17:36,600 --> 01:17:39,500
assume something that might seem simple,

1797
01:17:39,500 --> 01:17:41,560
but that might not be general, or at least I

1798
01:17:41,560 --> 01:17:44,040
don't see it as general, which is this ability

1799
01:17:44,040 --> 01:17:46,720
to engage in self-play, which you have by virtue

1800
01:17:46,720 --> 01:17:48,140
of being in the game environment,

1801
01:17:48,140 --> 01:17:49,840
but it might be hard to do.

1802
01:17:49,840 --> 01:17:52,400
So how are some ways that we can translate that

1803
01:17:52,400 --> 01:17:54,727
to more general examples of--

1804
01:17:54,727 --> 01:17:55,560
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

1805
01:17:55,560 --> 01:17:58,160
So this has interaction in a world.

1806
01:17:58,160 --> 01:18:00,337
There's a reward function the world is giving you.

1807
01:18:00,337 --> 01:18:02,920
How can we do this for something like just improving reasoning

1808
01:18:02,920 --> 01:18:03,753
in a language model?

1809
01:18:03,753 --> 01:18:05,320
I'll show you in a second.

1810
01:18:05,320 --> 01:18:07,360
But I'll just say that, yeah, there's a lot--

1811
01:18:07,360 --> 01:18:09,380
this is-- just all over reinforcement learning,

1812
01:18:09,380 --> 01:18:10,422
this is the thing you do.

1813
01:18:10,422 --> 01:18:12,120
This is how AlphaGo works.

1814
01:18:12,120 --> 01:18:15,280
You do some tree search over the possible moves you can make.

1815
01:18:15,280 --> 01:18:17,560
You find the best move according to the search.

1816
01:18:17,560 --> 01:18:20,680
And then you back up that solution

1817
01:18:20,680 --> 01:18:24,660
into a heuristic, which is able to predict-- without expanding

1818
01:18:24,660 --> 01:18:27,920
the tree, it's able to predict, if I were to expand the tree,

1819
01:18:27,920 --> 01:18:30,760
I would have found this solution, a good solution.

1820
01:18:30,760 --> 01:18:33,380
So therefore, that's a good node to be at.

1821
01:18:33,380 --> 01:18:35,200
I won't go into the details of this,

1822
01:18:35,200 --> 01:18:37,860
but it's a search process that then gets distilled back

1823
01:18:37,860 --> 01:18:39,200
into a learned heuristic.

1824
01:18:42,920 --> 01:18:44,420
Another one that does this-- and now

1825
01:18:44,420 --> 01:18:45,837
we're getting to how this can just

1826
01:18:45,837 --> 01:18:48,380
improve general-purpose language models and reasoning--

1827
01:18:48,380 --> 01:18:49,580
is called STaR.

1828
01:18:49,580 --> 01:18:53,300
So what STaR does is you start with a language model.

1829
01:18:53,300 --> 01:18:55,963
You ask a question.

1830
01:18:55,963 --> 01:18:57,880
It's prompted with a kind of chain of thought.

1831
01:18:57,880 --> 01:18:59,960
So it says, tell me your steps of thinking--

1832
01:18:59,960 --> 01:19:02,700
they call that a rationale-- and then produce an answer.

1833
01:19:02,700 --> 01:19:05,870
And it says, if I got the answer wrong, which I can check.

1834
01:19:05,870 --> 01:19:07,620
They assume they have some way of checking

1835
01:19:07,620 --> 01:19:10,620
if the answer is right or wrong, so they have a verifier here.

1836
01:19:10,620 --> 01:19:14,370
If I get the answer wrong, then I will--

1837
01:19:14,370 --> 01:19:15,120
I'll do something.

1838
01:19:15,120 --> 01:19:16,662
I won't talk about the bottom branch.

1839
01:19:16,662 --> 01:19:18,540
But if I get the answer right, then I

1840
01:19:18,540 --> 01:19:20,490
will reinforce that behavior.

1841
01:19:20,490 --> 01:19:22,793
I will take that answer and that rationale

1842
01:19:22,793 --> 01:19:24,710
and say, that was a good answer and rationale,

1843
01:19:24,710 --> 01:19:27,477
and now let me train my language model to preferentially produce

1844
01:19:27,477 --> 01:19:29,810
these good rationales, these good chains of thought that

1845
01:19:29,810 --> 01:19:31,797
lead to the good answer.

1846
01:19:31,797 --> 01:19:34,130
If they get it wrong, they also have this hint operation

1847
01:19:34,130 --> 01:19:35,850
that tries to give them some signal

1848
01:19:35,850 --> 01:19:39,670
that maybe that's not critical.

1849
01:19:39,670 --> 01:19:41,490
So this is what a lot of people seem

1850
01:19:41,490 --> 01:19:43,188
to think that this O1 model does.

1851
01:19:43,188 --> 01:19:45,730
So I'll leave-- I'll end the talk on this kind of speculation

1852
01:19:45,730 --> 01:19:47,850
of because this is the latest--

1853
01:19:47,850 --> 01:19:49,263
OpenAI thing.

1854
01:19:49,263 --> 01:19:50,930
But I think that we have a pretty good--

1855
01:19:50,930 --> 01:19:55,250
Sasha Rush gives this as one of his candidate explanations

1856
01:19:55,250 --> 01:19:57,730
for O1, but I feel like it's got to be something

1857
01:19:57,730 --> 01:19:58,830
basically like this.

1858
01:19:58,830 --> 01:20:01,793
So one is you search for chains of thoughts

1859
01:20:01,793 --> 01:20:03,210
that solve the problem, but you're

1860
01:20:03,210 --> 01:20:05,460
going to randomly sample different chains of thoughts.

1861
01:20:05,460 --> 01:20:07,010
So some chain of thought sample--

1862
01:20:07,010 --> 01:20:08,370
some-- it's like beam search.

1863
01:20:08,370 --> 01:20:10,770
I'm looking over all the possible different ways

1864
01:20:10,770 --> 01:20:12,890
the language model could start reasoning

1865
01:20:12,890 --> 01:20:15,370
and thinking aloud until it comes up with the answer.

1866
01:20:15,370 --> 01:20:18,323
And now I'm going to find the ones,

1867
01:20:18,323 --> 01:20:19,990
according to some scoring function, that

1868
01:20:19,990 --> 01:20:21,370
actually got the right answer.

1869
01:20:21,370 --> 01:20:23,790
And then I'll fine-tune my language model on--

1870
01:20:23,790 --> 01:20:28,190
to directly output those good chains of thought.

1871
01:20:28,190 --> 01:20:30,610
OK, how will I verify if I got the right answer?

1872
01:20:30,610 --> 01:20:32,120
It could be a learned verifier.

1873
01:20:32,120 --> 01:20:33,370
That's another language model.

1874
01:20:33,370 --> 01:20:37,430
Or it could be some kind of humans on the internet that

1875
01:20:37,430 --> 01:20:40,110
are saying, this is correct or that is incorrect,

1876
01:20:40,110 --> 01:20:43,510
or it could be a compiler or a proof checker that

1877
01:20:43,510 --> 01:20:46,810
actually symbolically checks if you're correct or incorrect.

1878
01:20:46,810 --> 01:20:48,230
We don't know.

1879
01:20:48,230 --> 01:20:49,243
And then I just repeat.

1880
01:20:49,243 --> 01:20:50,910
So now the LLM gets better at outputting

1881
01:20:50,910 --> 01:20:52,270
better chains of thought.

1882
01:20:52,270 --> 01:20:54,960
And that makes my search converge

1883
01:20:54,960 --> 01:20:57,210
on a narrower space of really good chains of thoughts.

1884
01:20:57,210 --> 01:20:59,390
But I still-- I can still sample different possibilities

1885
01:20:59,390 --> 01:21:01,510
in that space, see which ones are the best and repeat

1886
01:21:01,510 --> 01:21:02,010
the process.

1887
01:21:02,010 --> 01:21:05,390
It's just reinforcement learning applied to reasoning

1888
01:21:05,390 --> 01:21:07,030
with a language model.

1889
01:21:07,030 --> 01:21:08,990
And at test time, at deployment time,

1890
01:21:08,990 --> 01:21:11,170
you can just use your fine-tuned LLM,

1891
01:21:11,170 --> 01:21:14,650
or you can use your LLM plus additional search on top of it.

1892
01:21:14,650 --> 01:21:17,380
And that's why if you run O1 or any of the other models that

1893
01:21:17,380 --> 01:21:19,020
do this type of search, they will

1894
01:21:19,020 --> 01:21:21,460
take longer because they're doing extra search on top

1895
01:21:21,460 --> 01:21:22,520
of your solution.

1896
01:21:22,520 --> 01:21:25,500
That also improves things.

1897
01:21:25,500 --> 01:21:27,940
AUDIENCE: That feels like more rounds of searching, so

1898
01:21:27,940 --> 01:21:30,908
how could this be more efficient as they claim?

1899
01:21:30,908 --> 01:21:32,700
PHILLIP ISOLA: This feels like more round--

1900
01:21:32,700 --> 01:21:33,600
has it more efficient?

1901
01:21:33,600 --> 01:21:34,700
So they're not-- I think they're not

1902
01:21:34,700 --> 01:21:35,908
claiming it's more efficient.

1903
01:21:35,908 --> 01:21:37,820
They're claiming it achieves higher accuracy.

1904
01:21:37,820 --> 01:21:41,420
So by thinking more, by doing a lot more

1905
01:21:41,420 --> 01:21:43,140
compute on every single query to come up

1906
01:21:43,140 --> 01:21:44,620
with better and better answers, you

1907
01:21:44,620 --> 01:21:46,450
can distill that back into your base model.

1908
01:21:46,450 --> 01:21:48,200
That base model will be just as efficient.

1909
01:21:48,200 --> 01:21:50,075
But if you use that base model in conjunction

1910
01:21:50,075 --> 01:21:52,780
with additional search on top, you can always do as much search

1911
01:21:52,780 --> 01:21:53,280
as you want.

1912
01:21:53,280 --> 01:21:55,180
You could do infinite search on top.

1913
01:21:55,180 --> 01:21:57,780
Under some conditions, if you do Best-of-N

1914
01:21:57,780 --> 01:22:01,360
with a very uniform prior and you do infinite search,

1915
01:22:01,360 --> 01:22:03,240
then you'll be the best of all worlds.

1916
01:22:03,240 --> 01:22:05,100
And so this is just a way of using more

1917
01:22:05,100 --> 01:22:07,020
compute to get better answers.

1918
01:22:07,020 --> 01:22:08,082
Yep.

1919
01:22:08,082 --> 01:22:09,540
AUDIENCE: On the previous slide, it

1920
01:22:09,540 --> 01:22:13,920
just assumed a new training instead of question rationale

1921
01:22:13,920 --> 01:22:14,660
and answer.

1922
01:22:14,660 --> 01:22:19,120
Is there any idea on the scale of that training set that's

1923
01:22:19,120 --> 01:22:20,617
needed at this point?

1924
01:22:20,617 --> 01:22:22,200
PHILLIP ISOLA: I think that this might

1925
01:22:22,200 --> 01:22:25,020
have some supervised rationales or some seed,

1926
01:22:25,020 --> 01:22:27,840
but then it can generate its own-- the language model will

1927
01:22:27,840 --> 01:22:29,918
output a rationale, and the good rationales

1928
01:22:29,918 --> 01:22:32,460
that led to the correct answer get added to the training set.

1929
01:22:32,460 --> 01:22:34,480
So it generates its own rationales.

1930
01:22:34,480 --> 01:22:37,280
AUDIENCE: Do you know what they started at, though, to--

1931
01:22:37,280 --> 01:22:40,120
PHILLIP ISOLA: I don't remember, but I

1932
01:22:40,120 --> 01:22:44,080
think they only had a handful or some human-written rationales.

1933
01:22:44,080 --> 01:22:46,720
Yeah.

1934
01:22:46,720 --> 01:22:48,380
OK, cool.

1935
01:22:48,380 --> 01:22:52,282
So that's the latest, greatest thing in AI.

1936
01:22:52,282 --> 01:22:53,740
We don't know exactly how it works,

1937
01:22:53,740 --> 01:22:55,220
but it's very much a cool frontier.

1938
01:22:55,220 --> 01:22:57,590
So I will end there.
